  ("InformationTheory.Asymptotic module doc 0", "../.././",
   "# Asymptotic / exponent framework\x0a\x0aThe exponent-equality relation `\\doteq` of Cover–Thomas and a closed-form rate-extraction\x0awrapper, forming the asymptotic / rate API layer.\x0a\x0a## Notation\x0a\x0a* `a ≐ b` (`DotEq a b`): `(Real.log ∘ a − Real.log ∘ b) =o[atTop] (·:ℝ)`.\x0a* The textbook `f(n) = o(n)` is `f =o[atTop] (fun n : ℕ => (n : ℝ))`.\x0a* The textbook `f(n) = o(1)` is `f =o[atTop] (fun _ => (1 : ℝ))`.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `DotEq` — exponent equality, defined directly as an `IsLittleO`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `dotEq_iff_tendsto_log_div` — the bridge to `(1/n) · log (a/b) → 0` under positivity.\x0a* `exp_decay_N_of_pos` — a closed-form block length for `exp(-n·g) < ε'`.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0a* `DotEq` is `ℝ`-valued; no `ℝ≥0∞` alias is published here.\x0a* The notation `≐` is `scoped[InformationTheory.Asymptotic]`, so only call sites that\x0a  `open InformationTheory.Asymptotic` see it.\x0a* Positivity is not built into the predicate (Mathlib's `Real.log` returns `0` for `x ≤ 0`,\x0a  so `DotEq` is well-defined for any `ℕ → ℝ`); it is required at the bridge / `mul` / `inv`\x0a  use sites instead.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Asymptotic-exponent-framework\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Asymptotic / exponent framework <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Asymptotic-exponent-framework\">#</a></h1><p>The exponent-equality relation <code>\\doteq</code> of Cover–Thomas and a closed-form rate-extraction\x0awrapper, forming the asymptotic / rate API layer.</p><h2 id=\"Notation\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Notation <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Notation\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>a ≐ b</code> (<code>DotEq a b</code>): <code>(Real.log ∘ a − Real.log ∘ b) =o[atTop] (·:ℝ)</code>.</li><li>The textbook <code>f(n) = o(n)</code> is <code>f =o[atTop] (fun n : ℕ =&gt; (n : ℝ))</code>.</li><li>The textbook <code>f(n) = o(1)</code> is <code>f =o[atTop] (fun _ =&gt; (1 : ℝ))</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>DotEq</code> — exponent equality, defined directly as an <code>IsLittleO</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>dotEq_iff_tendsto_log_div</code> — the bridge to <code>(1/n) · log (a/b) → 0</code> under positivity.</li><li><code>exp_decay_N_of_pos</code> — a closed-form block length for <code>exp(-n·g) &lt; ε'</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Implementation-notes\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Implementation notes <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Implementation-notes\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>DotEq</code> is <code>ℝ</code>-valued; no <code>ℝ≥0∞</code> alias is published here.</li><li>The notation <code>≐</code> is <code>scoped[InformationTheory.Asymptotic]</code>, so only call sites that\x0a<code>open InformationTheory.Asymptotic</code> see it.</li><li>Positivity is not built into the predicate (Mathlib's <code>Real.log</code> returns <code>0</code> for <code>x ≤ 0</code>,\x0aso <code>DotEq</code> is well-defined for any <code>ℕ → ℝ</code>); it is required at the bridge / <code>mul</code> / <code>inv</code>\x0ause sites instead.</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Asymptotic.dotEq_iff_tendsto_log_div", ".././",
   "Bridge: under positivity, `a ≐ b` is equivalent to\x0a`Tendsto (fun n => (1/n) * log (a n / b n)) atTop (𝓝 0)`. ",
   "<p>Bridge: under positivity, <code>a ≐ b</code> is equivalent to\x0a<code>Tendsto (fun n =&gt; (1/n) * log (a n / b n)) atTop (𝓝 0)</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.FiniteJointPMF.errorProb", ".././",
   "Error probability of a Markov-form joint PMF on `(X, Xh)` where the\x0asecond coordinate plays the role of the estimator `Xh`. The error event is\x0a`{(x, xh) : x ≠ xh}`. ",
   "<p>Error probability of a Markov-form joint PMF on <code>(X, Xh)</code> where the\x0asecond coordinate plays the role of the estimator <code>Xh</code>. The error event is\x0a<code>{(x, xh) : x ≠ xh}</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Joint3.marginalY", "../.././",
   "Marginal mass on `Y`, summing out both `X` and `E`. ",
   "<p>Marginal mass on <code>Y</code>, summing out both <code>X</code> and <code>E</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.FiniteJointPMF.fano_inequality", "./",
   "**Fano's inequality** (Markov form, textbook RHS):\x0a`H(X | Xh) ≤ binEntropy Pe + Pe · log(|X| - 1)`, with no external `hcore`\x0ahypothesis. ",
   "<p><strong>Fano's inequality</strong> (Markov form, textbook RHS):\x0a<code>H(X | Xh) ≤ binEntropy Pe + Pe · log(|X| - 1)</code>, with no external <code>hcore</code>\x0ahypothesis.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Fano.Measure module doc 0", ".././",
   "# Fano's inequality: measure-theoretic form\x0a\x0aThe measure-theoretic form of Fano's inequality (Cover–Thomas, Polyanskiy), proved with\x0aMathlib's `condDistrib` (the regular conditional distribution) as the central object, for a\x0adeterministic decoder `Y → X`.\x0a\x0aSetting:\x0a* `X : Fintype` (discrete, finite) — the channel alphabet\x0a* `Y : MeasurableSpace` (arbitrary; `ℝ`, `ℝⁿ`, Polish spaces, etc. are admissible)\x0a* random variables `Xs : Ω → X` (source) and `Yo : Ω → Y` (observation) on `(Ω, μ)`\x0a* `decoder : Y → X` — a deterministic measurable decoder\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `condEntropy μ Xs Yo` — the conditional entropy `H(Xs | Yo)` as an integral over `μ.map Yo`.\x0a* `errorProb μ Xs Yo decoder` — the decoding error probability `P(Xs ≠ decoder ∘ Yo)`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `fano_inequality_measure_theoretic` — `H(Xs | Yo) ≤ binEntropy Pe + Pe · log(|X| − 1)`.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aThe discrete Fano inequality of `Fano.Core` is applied pointwise for each `y : Y`, then\x0aaggregated into integral form on `P_Yo = μ.map Yo` via Bochner–Jensen. The proof chains four\x0asteps:\x0a\x0a```\x0aH(Xs | Yo)\x0a  = ∫ y, [∑ x, negMulLog (Q_y {x})] dP_Yo                    -- def of condEntropy\x0a  ≤ ∫ y, qaryEntropy |X| (Pe_y) dP_Yo                        -- Step 1: pointwise_fano\x0a  ≤ qaryEntropy |X| (∫ y, Pe_y dP_Yo)                        -- Step 2: Bochner Jensen\x0a  = qaryEntropy |X| (errorProb μ Xs Yo decoder)              -- Step 3: disintegration\x0a  = h(Pe) + Pe · log(|X| - 1)                                -- Step 4: qaryEntropy split\x0a```\x0a\x0aHere `Q_y = (condDistrib Xs Yo μ y).real` is the conditional distribution of `Xs` given `y`\x0aand `Pe_y = Q_y {x | x ≠ decoder y}` is the error rate given `y`. The `StandardBorelSpace`\x0arequirement of `condDistrib` is imposed on the output type, which here is `X`; from\x0a`Fintype + MeasurableSingletonClass + Countable` the instance\x0a`DiscreteMeasurableSpace → StandardBorelSpace` is derived automatically, so `Y` carries no\x0aextra constraint.\x0a\x0a## References\x0a\x0a* T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, *Elements of Information Theory* (2nd ed.), Wiley, 2006.\x0a* Y. Polyanskiy and Y. Wu, *Information Theory: From Coding to Learning*, Cambridge, 2024.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Fano-s-inequality-measure-theoretic-form\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Fano's inequality: measure-theoretic form <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Fano-s-inequality-measure-theoretic-form\">#</a></h1><p>The measure-theoretic form of Fano's inequality (Cover–Thomas, Polyanskiy), proved with\x0aMathlib's <code>condDistrib</code> (the regular conditional distribution) as the central object, for a\x0adeterministic decoder <code>Y → X</code>.</p><p>Setting:</p><ul><li><code>X : Fintype</code> (discrete, finite) — the channel alphabet</li><li><code>Y : MeasurableSpace</code> (arbitrary; <code>ℝ</code>, <code>ℝⁿ</code>, Polish spaces, etc. are admissible)</li><li>random variables <code>Xs : Ω → X</code> (source) and <code>Yo : Ω → Y</code> (observation) on <code>(Ω, μ)</code></li><li><code>decoder : Y → X</code> — a deterministic measurable decoder</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>condEntropy μ Xs Yo</code> — the conditional entropy <code>H(Xs | Yo)</code> as an integral over <code>μ.map Yo</code>.</li><li><code>errorProb μ Xs Yo decoder</code> — the decoding error probability <code>P(Xs ≠ decoder ∘ Yo)</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>fano_inequality_measure_theoretic</code> — <code>H(Xs | Yo) ≤ binEntropy Pe + Pe · log(|X| − 1)</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Implementation-notes\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Implementation notes <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Implementation-notes\">#</a></h2><p>The discrete Fano inequality of <code>Fano.Core</code> is applied pointwise for each <code>y : Y</code>, then\x0aaggregated into integral form on <code>P_Yo = μ.map Yo</code> via Bochner–Jensen. The proof chains four\x0asteps:</p><pre><code>H(Xs | Yo)\x0a  = ∫ y, [∑ x, negMulLog (Q_y {x})] dP_Yo                    -- def of condEntropy\x0a  ≤ ∫ y, qaryEntropy |X| (Pe_y) dP_Yo                        -- Step 1: pointwise_fano\x0a  ≤ qaryEntropy |X| (∫ y, Pe_y dP_Yo)                        -- Step 2: Bochner Jensen\x0a  = qaryEntropy |X| (errorProb μ Xs Yo decoder)              -- Step 3: disintegration\x0a  = h(Pe) + Pe · log(|X| - 1)                                -- Step 4: qaryEntropy split\x0a</code></pre><p>Here <code>Q_y = (condDistrib Xs Yo μ y).real</code> is the conditional distribution of <code>Xs</code> given <code>y</code>\x0aand <code>Pe_y = Q_y {x | x ≠ decoder y}</code> is the error rate given <code>y</code>. The <code>StandardBorelSpace</code>\x0arequirement of <code>condDistrib</code> is imposed on the output type, which here is <code>X</code>; from\x0a<code>Fintype + MeasurableSingletonClass + Countable</code> the instance\x0a<code>DiscreteMeasurableSpace → StandardBorelSpace</code> is derived automatically, so <code>Y</code> carries no\x0aextra constraint.</p><h2 id=\"References\" class=\"markdown-heading\">References <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#References\">#</a></h2><ul><li>T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, <em>Elements of Information Theory</em> (2nd ed.), Wiley, 2006.</li><li>Y. Polyanskiy and Y. Wu, <em>Information Theory: From Coding to Learning</em>, Cambridge, 2024.</li></ul>"),
