  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TwoSided.pmfLogCondInfty", "./",
   "Limit log-likelihood (conditional on the full backward tail). ",
   "<p>Limit log-likelihood (conditional on the full backward tail).</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TwoSided.ergodic_shiftZ", ".././",
   "The two-sided shift is ergodic when the underlying process is ergodic.\x0a\x0aBy cylinder approximation + ℕ-factor transfer. For any\x0a`shiftZ`-invariant measurable set `A`, approximate `A` to within ε by a\x0ameasurable cylinder, then shift to move the index set into the nonnegative\x0ahalf-line. This yields a `posSigma`-measurable approximation, so `A` is μZ-a.e.\x0aequal to a `posSigma`-measurable set `A' = natProj⁻¹ B`. By ergodicity of\x0a`shiftN` on `μ.map forwardEmbed`, `B` is either null or co-null, hence so is `A`. ",
   "<p>The two-sided shift is ergodic when the underlying process is ergodic.</p><p>By cylinder approximation + ℕ-factor transfer. For any\x0a<code>shiftZ</code>-invariant measurable set <code>A</code>, approximate <code>A</code> to within ε by a\x0ameasurable cylinder, then shift to move the index set into the nonnegative\x0ahalf-line. This yields a <code>posSigma</code>-measurable approximation, so <code>A</code> is μZ-a.e.\x0aequal to a <code>posSigma</code>-measurable set <code>A' = natProj⁻¹ B</code>. By ergodicity of\x0a<code>shiftN</code> on <code>μ.map forwardEmbed</code>, <code>B</code> is either null or co-null, hence so is <code>A</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TwoSided.forwardEmbed_semiconj", "../.././",
   "The forward embedding semiconjugates `T` and `shiftN`. ",
   "<p>The forward embedding semiconjugates <code>T</code> and <code>shiftN</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AEP.Basic.Achievability module doc 4", "./",
   "### Point-wise probability upper bound on the typical set\x0a\x0aFor any `x ∈ T_ε^n`, `P^n(x) = ∏ P(x_i) ≤ exp(-n(H - ε))`. This is the\x0apoint-wise companion of the size bound `|T_ε^n| ≤ exp(n(H+ε))`.\x0a\x0aThe factorization `μ.map (jointRV Xs n) = Measure.pi (μ.map (Xs ·))` requires\x0amutual independence (`iIndepFun`), not just pairwise independence. ",
   "<h3 id=\"Point-wise-probability-upper-bound-on-the-typical-set\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Point-wise probability upper bound on the typical set <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Point-wise-probability-upper-bound-on-the-typical-set\">#</a></h3><p>For any <code>x ∈ T_ε^n</code>, <code>P^n(x) = ∏ P(x_i) ≤ exp(-n(H - ε))</code>. This is the\x0apoint-wise companion of the size bound <code>|T_ε^n| ≤ exp(n(H+ε))</code>.</p><p>The factorization <code>μ.map (jointRV Xs n) = Measure.pi (μ.map (Xs ·))</code> requires\x0amutual independence (<code>iIndepFun</code>), not just pairwise independence.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AEP.Basic.Core module doc 2", ".././",
   "### Typical set `T_ε^n` ",
   "<h3 id=\"Typical-set-T-ε^n\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Typical set <code>T_ε^n</code> <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Typical-set-T-ε^n\">#</a></h3>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.channelCoding_E2_lt_of_rate", "../.././",
   "Closed-form `N(I, R, ε, ε')` for the channel-coding E2 term. Given the AEP\x0agap `g := I - R - 3ε > 0` and any tolerance `ε' > 0`, there is `N` such that\x0a`(⌈exp(n·R)⌉ - 1) · exp(n · (-I + 3ε)) < ε'` for all `n ≥ N`. ",
   "<p>Closed-form <code>N(I, R, ε, ε')</code> for the channel-coding E2 term. Given the AEP\x0agap <code>g := I - R - 3ε &gt; 0</code> and any tolerance <code>ε' &gt; 0</code>, there is <code>N</code> such that\x0a<code>(⌈exp(n·R)⌉ - 1) · exp(n · (-I + 3ε)) &lt; ε'</code> for all <code>n ≥ N</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.AchievabilityExpurgation module doc 0", "./",
   "# Expurgation, power-constraint witness, and code extraction\x0a\x0aThe expurgation and feasibility apparatus for AWGN achievability (Cover–Thomas\x0a9.2): worst-half expurgation, the power-constraint slack witness producing a\x0astrictly smaller variance, and the bridge from a deterministic codebook to the\x0a`AwgnCode` type.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `awgn_exists_codebook_le_avg` — a codebook attaining the average error bound.\x0a* `awgn_expurgate_worst_half` — worst-half expurgation keeps `M/2` good indices.\x0a* `awgnPowerWitness_exists` — a strictly smaller variance `P' < P` with `R`\x0a  still below `capacity(P')`.\x0a* `awgn_extract_AwgnCode` — packages a deterministic codebook into an `AwgnCode`.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Expurgation-power-constraint-witness-and-code-extraction\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Expurgation, power-constraint witness, and code extraction <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Expurgation-power-constraint-witness-and-code-extraction\">#</a></h1><p>The expurgation and feasibility apparatus for AWGN achievability (Cover–Thomas\x0a9.2): worst-half expurgation, the power-constraint slack witness producing a\x0astrictly smaller variance, and the bridge from a deterministic codebook to the\x0a<code>AwgnCode</code> type.</p><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>awgn_exists_codebook_le_avg</code> — a codebook attaining the average error bound.</li><li><code>awgn_expurgate_worst_half</code> — worst-half expurgation keeps <code>M/2</code> good indices.</li><li><code>awgnPowerWitness_exists</code> — a strictly smaller variance <code>P' &lt; P</code> with <code>R</code>\x0astill below <code>capacity(P')</code>.</li><li><code>awgn_extract_AwgnCode</code> — packages a deterministic codebook into an <code>AwgnCode</code>.</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.awgnCapacity", ".././",
   "Power-constrained channel capacity. Supremum of `I(p; W)` over probability\x0ameasures `p` in `awgnPowerConstraintSet P` (second moment ≤ `P`, lintegral form). ",
   "<p>Power-constrained channel capacity. Supremum of <code>I(p; W)</code> over probability\x0ameasures <code>p</code> in <code>awgnPowerConstraintSet P</code> (second moment ≤ <code>P</code>, lintegral form).</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.CapacityConverseMaxent module doc 8", "../.././",
   "## Capacity closed form ",
   "<h2 id=\"Capacity-closed-form\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Capacity closed form <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Capacity-closed-form\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.awgn_capacity_closed_form_of_isAwgnChannelMeasurable", "./",
   "The AWGN capacity closed form, re-published with the kernel-measurability\x0ahypothesis discharged by `isAwgnChannelMeasurable N`. The remaining hypotheses\x0a(`h_bridge_gauss`, `h_bdd`, `h_max_ent`) are unchanged.\x0a\x0a`@audit:superseded-by(awgn_capacity_closed_form_genuine)` ",
   "<p>The AWGN capacity closed form, re-published with the kernel-measurability\x0ahypothesis discharged by <code>isAwgnChannelMeasurable N</code>. The remaining hypotheses\x0a(<code>h_bridge_gauss</code>, <code>h_bdd</code>, <code>h_max_ent</code>) are unchanged.</p><p><code>@audit:superseded-by(awgn_capacity_closed_form_genuine)</code></p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.awgn_per_letter_mi_le_log_var", ".././",
   "Per-letter mutual-information bound via the per-letter input variance:\x0a`I(Xᵢ; Yᵢ) ≤ (1/2) log(1 + perLetterInputSecondMoment c i / N)`, derived from the Gaussian\x0amaximum-entropy bound `differentialEntropy_le_gaussian_of_variance_le`. The variance of `Yᵢ`\x0ais at most `E[Xᵢ²] + N` since the input and the noise are independent, giving\x0a```\x0aI(Xᵢ; Yᵢ) = h(Yᵢ) − h(gaussianReal 0 N)              -- bridge hypothesis\x0a          ≤ (1/2) log(2πe·v_Y) − (1/2) log(2πe·N)    -- Gaussian maximum entropy\x0a          = (1/2) log(v_Y / N) ≤ (1/2) log((S² + N)/N)\x0a          = (1/2) log(1 + S²/N)\x0a```\x0awith `v_Y := (perLetterInputSecondMoment c i + N).toNNReal`. ",
   "<p>Per-letter mutual-information bound via the per-letter input variance:\x0a<code>I(Xᵢ; Yᵢ) ≤ (1/2) log(1 + perLetterInputSecondMoment c i / N)</code>, derived from the Gaussian\x0amaximum-entropy bound <code>differentialEntropy_le_gaussian_of_variance_le</code>. The variance of <code>Yᵢ</code>\x0ais at most <code>E[Xᵢ²] + N</code> since the input and the noise are independent, giving</p><pre><code>I(Xᵢ; Yᵢ) = h(Yᵢ) − h(gaussianReal 0 N)              -- bridge hypothesis\x0a          ≤ (1/2) log(2πe·v_Y) − (1/2) log(2πe·N)    -- Gaussian maximum entropy\x0a          = (1/2) log(v_Y / N) ≤ (1/2) log((S² + N)/N)\x0a          = (1/2) log(1 + S²/N)\x0a</code></pre><p>with <code>v_Y := (perLetterInputSecondMoment c i + N).toNNReal</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.converseJointInline.instIsProbabilityMeasure", "../.././",
   "`converseJointInline` is a probability measure for `M ≥ 1` (mixture with weights\x0a`1/M` summing to 1). Mirror of `awgnConverseJoint.instIsProbabilityMeasure`\x0a(`ConverseMutualInfoFiniteness.lean:74`); needed so `IsMarkovChain`'s `[IsFiniteMeasure μ]`\x0aprerequisite resolves on the inlined joint. ",
   "<p><code>converseJointInline</code> is a probability measure for <code>M ≥ 1</code> (mixture with weights\x0a<code>1/M</code> summing to 1). Mirror of <code>awgnConverseJoint.instIsProbabilityMeasure</code>\x0a(<code>ConverseMutualInfoFiniteness.lean:74</code>); needed so <code>IsMarkovChain</code>'s <code>[IsFiniteMeasure μ]</code>\x0aprerequisite resolves on the inlined joint.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.JointlyTypicalSet module doc 0", "./",
   "# AWGN jointly typical set\x0a\x0aThe continuous jointly typical set for the AWGN channel coding theorem\x0a(Cover–Thomas Ch. 9).\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `AWGNJointlyTypicalSet n P N ε` — the jointly typical set on `ℝⁿ × ℝⁿ` cut out by\x0a  three empirical power bounds.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `AWGNJointlyTypicalSet_zero`, `AWGNJointlyTypicalSet_subset_of_le_ε`,\x0a  `AWGNJointlyTypicalSet_measurable` — degenerate case, monotonicity in the slack,\x0a  and measurability of the jointly typical set.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"AWGN-jointly-typical-set\" class=\"markdown-heading\">AWGN jointly typical set <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#AWGN-jointly-typical-set\">#</a></h1><p>The continuous jointly typical set for the AWGN channel coding theorem\x0a(Cover–Thomas Ch. 9).</p><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>AWGNJointlyTypicalSet n P N ε</code> — the jointly typical set on <code>ℝⁿ × ℝⁿ</code> cut out by\x0athree empirical power bounds.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>AWGNJointlyTypicalSet_zero</code>, <code>AWGNJointlyTypicalSet_subset_of_le_ε</code>,\x0a<code>AWGNJointlyTypicalSet_measurable</code> — degenerate case, monotonicity in the slack,\x0aand measurability of the jointly typical set.</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.AWGN.IsAwgnOutputGaussian", ".././",
   "The channel output marginal under Gaussian input `gaussianReal 0 P` equals the\x0aconvolution `gaussianReal 0 (P + N)`. ",
   "<p>The channel output marginal under Gaussian input <code>gaussianReal 0 P</code> equals the\x0aconvolution <code>gaussianReal 0 (P + N)</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BackwardFiltration module doc 0", "../.././",
   "# Backward filtration and tail σ-algebra\x0a\x0aGiven a measurable transformation `T : Ω → Ω` on a measurable space `(Ω, m₀)`,\x0athe *backward filtration* is the antitone sequence\x0a\x0a```\x0aℋ_n := T⁻ⁿ(m₀) = MeasurableSpace.comap (T^[n]) m₀\x0a```\x0a\x0aIndexed over `ℕᵒᵈ`, this becomes a `MeasureTheory.Filtration ℕᵒᵈ m₀`. The tail\x0aσ-algebra is `ℋ_∞ := ⋂_n ℋ_n`.\x0a\x0aThese objects underpin the backward martingale approach to Birkhoff's ergodic\x0atheorem.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `backwardFiltration` — the antitone sequence `n ↦ comap (T^[n]) m₀`\x0a  packaged as a `Filtration ℕᵒᵈ m₀`.\x0a* `tailSigma` — the tail σ-algebra `⨅ n, comap (T^[n]) m₀`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `backwardFiltration_apply` — applied form (definitional).\x0a* `tailSigma_le_comap_iterate` — `tailSigma ≤ comap (T^[n]) m₀` for every `n`.\x0a* `comap_T_tailSigma_le` — `comap T (tailSigma) ≤ tailSigma` (one half of\x0a  T-invariance; the equality direction requires extra structure such as\x0a  measure preservation).\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Backward-filtration-and-tail-σ-algebra\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Backward filtration and tail σ-algebra <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Backward-filtration-and-tail-σ-algebra\">#</a></h1><p>Given a measurable transformation <code>T : Ω → Ω</code> on a measurable space <code>(Ω, m₀)</code>,\x0athe <em>backward filtration</em> is the antitone sequence</p><pre><code>ℋ_n := T⁻ⁿ(m₀) = MeasurableSpace.comap (T^[n]) m₀\x0a</code></pre><p>Indexed over <code>ℕᵒᵈ</code>, this becomes a <code>MeasureTheory.Filtration ℕᵒᵈ m₀</code>. The tail\x0aσ-algebra is <code>ℋ_∞ := ⋂_n ℋ_n</code>.</p><p>These objects underpin the backward martingale approach to Birkhoff's ergodic\x0atheorem.</p><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>backwardFiltration</code> — the antitone sequence <code>n ↦ comap (T^[n]) m₀</code>\x0apackaged as a <code>Filtration ℕᵒᵈ m₀</code>.</li><li><code>tailSigma</code> — the tail σ-algebra <code>⨅ n, comap (T^[n]) m₀</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>backwardFiltration_apply</code> — applied form (definitional).</li><li><code>tailSigma_le_comap_iterate</code> — <code>tailSigma ≤ comap (T^[n]) m₀</code> for every <code>n</code>.</li><li><code>comap_T_tailSigma_le</code> — <code>comap T (tailSigma) ≤ tailSigma</code> (one half of\x0aT-invariance; the equality direction requires extra structure such as\x0ameasure preservation).</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BirkhoffErgodic module doc 3", "./",
   "## §3 Birkhoff sum recursion ",
   "<h2 id=\"3-Birkhoff-sum-recursion\" class=\"markdown-heading\">§3 Birkhoff sum recursion <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#3-Birkhoff-sum-recursion\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.integral_comp_iterate_eq", ".././",
   "Each term `f ∘ T^[i]` has the same integral as `f`. ",
