  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BoolLaw module doc 2", "../.././",
   "## The entropy of the two-point law ",
   "<h2 id=\"The-entropy-of-the-two-point-law\" class=\"markdown-heading\">The entropy of the two-point law <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#The-entropy-of-the-two-point-law\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.bcAmbient_pairwise_coord", "./",
   "Pairwise independence of any BC coordinate selector under the ambient measure. ",
   "<p>Pairwise independence of any BC coordinate selector under the ambient measure.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.bc_chan_fold_UY₂_set", ".././",
   "Channel fold on the `(U, Y₂)` axes, in joint form: the joint `(U, Y₂)`-block law of a\x0afinite set `T` equals the cloud/satellite/channel average of the `β₂`-projected channel mass,\x0aretaining the cloud block `u` inside the slice.  Derived from the master fold by projecting\x0aout `X` and the `β₁`-output while keeping `U`.  This is the receiver-2 correct-cloud analytic\x0acore, where the transmitted cloud both indexes the slice and steers the satellite (the\x0a`U`-preserving counterpart of `bc_chan_fold_Y₂_set`). ",
   "<p>Channel fold on the <code>(U, Y₂)</code> axes, in joint form: the joint <code>(U, Y₂)</code>-block law of a\x0afinite set <code>T</code> equals the cloud/satellite/channel average of the <code>β₂</code>-projected channel mass,\x0aretaining the cloud block <code>u</code> inside the slice.  Derived from the master fold by projecting\x0aout <code>X</code> and the <code>β₁</code>-output while keeping <code>U</code>.  This is the receiver-2 correct-cloud analytic\x0acore, where the transmitted cloud both indexes the slice and steers the satellite (the\x0a<code>U</code>-preserving counterpart of <code>bc_chan_fold_Y₂_set</code>).</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Achievability.Setup module doc 4", "../.././",
   "### Two-tier (cloud / satellite) random codebook ",
   "<h3 id=\"Two-tier-cloud-satellite-random-codebook\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Two-tier (cloud / satellite) random codebook <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Two-tier-cloud-satellite-random-codebook\">#</a></h3>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.bcJointDistribution", "./",
   "The per-coordinate broadcast joint law on `U × α × β₁ × β₂`: the compProd chain\x0a`pU → K → W` (`U ∼ pU`, `X ∣ U ∼ K`, `(Y₁, Y₂) ∣ X ∼ W`), reshaped from the left-nested\x0a`(U × α) × (β₁ × β₂)` to the right-nested quadruple. ",
   "<p>The per-coordinate broadcast joint law on <code>U × α × β₁ × β₂</code>: the compProd chain\x0a<code>pU → K → W</code> (<code>U ∼ pU</code>, <code>X ∣ U ∼ K</code>, <code>(Y₁, Y₂) ∣ X ∼ W</code>), reshaped from the left-nested\x0a<code>(U × α) × (β₁ × β₂)</code> to the right-nested quadruple.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.InBCCapacityRegion", ".././",
   "The degraded-BC capacity-region predicate: a bundle of the two corner inequalities\x0a`R₁ ≤ I₁`, `R₂ ≤ I₂` on four real numbers. The slots `I₁, I₂` are abstract information bounds\x0a— the predicate does **not** fix their meaning. Two instantiations are intended:\x0a\x0a* **message level** (`bc_converse_message_level`): `I₁ = I(W₁; (W₂, Y₁ⁿ))`,\x0a  `I₂ = I(W₂; Y₂ⁿ)` plus Fano terms.\x0a* **single letter** (the standard Cover–Thomas Thm 15.6.2 form, `bc_degraded_converse`): with an\x0a  auxiliary `U`, `I₁ = ∑ᵢ I(Xᵢ; Y_{1,i} | Uᵢ)`, `I₂ = ∑ᵢ I(Uᵢ; Y_{2,i})`.\x0a\x0aUnlike the symmetric MAC region, the two receivers are asymmetric (receiver 2 is the degraded\x0aone), so there is no role-swap symmetry — only monotonicity in the information bounds\x0a(`InBCCapacityRegion.mono`). ",
   "<p>The degraded-BC capacity-region predicate: a bundle of the two corner inequalities\x0a<code>R₁ ≤ I₁</code>, <code>R₂ ≤ I₂</code> on four real numbers. The slots <code>I₁, I₂</code> are abstract information bounds\x0a— the predicate does <strong>not</strong> fix their meaning. Two instantiations are intended:</p><ul><li><strong>message level</strong> (<code>bc_converse_message_level</code>): <code>I₁ = I(W₁; (W₂, Y₁ⁿ))</code>,\x0a<code>I₂ = I(W₂; Y₂ⁿ)</code> plus Fano terms.</li><li><strong>single letter</strong> (the standard Cover–Thomas Thm 15.6.2 form, <code>bc_degraded_converse</code>): with an\x0aauxiliary <code>U</code>, <code>I₁ = ∑ᵢ I(Xᵢ; Y_{1,i} | Uᵢ)</code>, <code>I₂ = ∑ᵢ I(Uᵢ; Y_{2,i})</code>.</li></ul><p>Unlike the symmetric MAC region, the two receivers are asymmetric (receiver 2 is the degraded\x0aone), so there is no role-swap symmetry — only monotonicity in the information bounds\x0a(<code>InBCCapacityRegion.mono</code>).</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Converse module doc 2", "../.././",
   "## Message-level Fano bounds\x0a\x0aThe message-level bounds are pure single-shot Fano plumbing on the output measurable spaces,\x0aso the finite-alphabet / standard-Borel structure of the variable context is unused here. ",
   "<h2 id=\"Message-level-Fano-bounds\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Message-level Fano bounds <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Message-level-Fano-bounds\">#</a></h2><p>The message-level bounds are pure single-shot Fano plumbing on the output measurable spaces,\x0aso the finite-alphabet / standard-Borel structure of the variable context is unused here.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.marton_achievability", "./",
   "Achievability of Marton's inner bound for a two-receiver broadcast channel with private\x0amessages.  For auxiliary variables with joint law `pV`, an input kernel `K` and a channel `W`, a\x0arate pair obeying the three strict inequalities\x0a\x0a* `R₁ < I(V₁; Y₁)`,\x0a* `R₂ < I(V₂; Y₂)`,\x0a* `R₁ + R₂ < I(V₁; Y₁) + I(V₂; Y₂) − I(V₁; V₂)`\x0a\x0ais achievable: at every large enough block length there is a `BroadcastCode` whose two average\x0aerror probabilities are both below any prescribed `ε'`.\x0a\x0aThe input is a general kernel `K : Kernel (V₁ × V₂) α` rather than a deterministic map\x0a`x = f(v₁, v₂)`, so the textbook statement for a deterministic input is not a direct corollary:\x0aa deterministic kernel puts no mass on inputs off its image and therefore violates the\x0afull-support hypothesis `hK` that every typicality estimate in this development requires.  The\x0arate region is the same either way.\x0a\x0aThe hypotheses `hpV`, `hK` and `hW` are full-support regularity preconditions, shared with\x0a`marton_strong_mutual_covering` and with `bc_achievability`, and carry no part of the coding\x0aargument.  No positivity of the rates is assumed: a nonpositive rate only asks for a single\x0amessage, `⌈exp (n R)⌉₊ = 1`, which the construction supplies as well.\x0a\x0aNo typicality radius appears in the statement, and the construction uses three nested ones rather\x0athan a single shared one: the two decoders test weak joint typicality at a radius `ε`, the\x0atransmitted blocks are pinned at the strictly smaller `martonStrongRadius`, and the encoder\x0aselects a pair pinned at the smaller `martonCoveringRadius` again.\x0a\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>Achievability of Marton's inner bound for a two-receiver broadcast channel with private\x0amessages.  For auxiliary variables with joint law <code>pV</code>, an input kernel <code>K</code> and a channel <code>W</code>, a\x0arate pair obeying the three strict inequalities</p><ul><li><code>R₁ &lt; I(V₁; Y₁)</code>,</li><li><code>R₂ &lt; I(V₂; Y₂)</code>,</li><li><code>R₁ + R₂ &lt; I(V₁; Y₁) + I(V₂; Y₂) − I(V₁; V₂)</code></li></ul><p>is achievable: at every large enough block length there is a <code>BroadcastCode</code> whose two average\x0aerror probabilities are both below any prescribed <code>ε'</code>.</p><p>The input is a general kernel <code>K : Kernel (V₁ × V₂) α</code> rather than a deterministic map\x0a<code>x = f(v₁, v₂)</code>, so the textbook statement for a deterministic input is not a direct corollary:\x0aa deterministic kernel puts no mass on inputs off its image and therefore violates the\x0afull-support hypothesis <code>hK</code> that every typicality estimate in this development requires.  The\x0arate region is the same either way.</p><p>The hypotheses <code>hpV</code>, <code>hK</code> and <code>hW</code> are full-support regularity preconditions, shared with\x0a<code>marton_strong_mutual_covering</code> and with <code>bc_achievability</code>, and carry no part of the coding\x0aargument.  No positivity of the rates is assumed: a nonpositive rate only asks for a single\x0amessage, <code>⌈exp (n R)⌉₊ = 1</code>, which the construction supplies as well.</p><p>No typicality radius appears in the statement, and the construction uses three nested ones rather\x0athan a single shared one: the two decoders test weak joint typicality at a radius <code>ε</code>, the\x0atransmitted blocks are pinned at the strictly smaller <code>martonStrongRadius</code>, and the encoder\x0aselects a pair pinned at the smaller <code>martonCoveringRadius</code> again.</p><p>@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonCoveringBandConst", ".././",
   "The Lipschitz factor relating the type radius of a jointly strongly typical auxiliary pair to\x0athe weak bands of the two auxiliary blocks and of their joint sequence.  It is the covering\x0acounterpart of `martonBandConst`, which governs the transmitted `(V₁, X)` pair instead.\x0a\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>The Lipschitz factor relating the type radius of a jointly strongly typical auxiliary pair to\x0athe weak bands of the two auxiliary blocks and of their joint sequence.  It is the covering\x0acounterpart of <code>martonBandConst</code>, which governs the transmitted <code>(V₁, X)</code> pair instead.</p><p>@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonSubcodebookMeasure", "../.././",
   "The subcodebook law: `p`-i.i.d. over all `M · M' · n` auxiliary letters, read as a codebook\x0awhose alphabet is the set of length-`n` words. ",
   "<p>The subcodebook law: <code>p</code>-i.i.d. over all <code>M · M' · n</code> auxiliary letters, read as a codebook\x0awhose alphabet is the set of length-<code>n</code> words.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.MarkovCore.Receiver1 module doc 1", "./",
   "### Coordinate laws of the Marton ambient measure — receiver 1 ",
   "<h3 id=\"Coordinate-laws-of-the-Marton-ambient-measure-receiver-1\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Coordinate laws of the Marton ambient measure — receiver 1 <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Coordinate-laws-of-the-Marton-ambient-measure-receiver-1\">#</a></h3>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonCoveringRadius₂", ".././",
   "The type radius at which the selected auxiliary pair has to be pinned for the transmitted\x0a`(V₂, X)` block to be pinned at `martonStrongRadius₂`.  It mirrors `martonCoveringRadius` with the\x0areceiver-2 band constant; an assembly that needs both pins at once takes the minimum of the two\x0aradii and reopens each of them with `jointStronglyTypicalSet_mono_radius`.\x0a\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>The type radius at which the selected auxiliary pair has to be pinned for the transmitted\x0a<code>(V₂, X)</code> block to be pinned at <code>martonStrongRadius₂</code>.  It mirrors <code>martonCoveringRadius</code> with the\x0areceiver-2 band constant; an assembly that needs both pins at once takes the minimum of the two\x0aradii and reopens each of them with <code>jointStronglyTypicalSet_mono_radius</code>.</p><p>@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonWeightedSum_eq_auxWeightObjective_add_aggregate", "../.././",
   "The weighted sum of the three informations of the region inequalities is the auxiliary-weight\x0aobjective at the weight vector of the outer auxiliary marginal, plus a term reading the weights\x0aonly through the channel-input aggregate. ",
   "<p>The weighted sum of the three informations of the region inequalities is the auxiliary-weight\x0aobjective at the weight vector of the outer auxiliary marginal, plus a term reading the weights\x0aonly through the channel-input aggregate.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonAuxRow", "./",
   "The row, indexed by the outer auxiliary letter `u`, of the stochastic matrix that carries a\x0aweight vector on `V₁` to the induced law on the channel input: the mass `u` contributes to the\x0ainput letter `x` after the inner auxiliary letter and the channel input have been generated. ",
   "<p>The row, indexed by the outer auxiliary letter <code>u</code>, of the stochastic matrix that carries a\x0aweight vector on <code>V₁</code> to the induced law on the channel input: the mass <code>u</code> contributes to the\x0ainput letter <code>x</code> after the inner auxiliary letter and the channel input have been generated.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonV₂s", ".././",
   "The second auxiliary coordinate `ω ↦ (ω i).2.1`. ",
   "<p>The second auxiliary coordinate <code>ω ↦ (ω i).2.1</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Marton.martonRegionUnion_subset_uv", "../.././",
   "Marton's inner bound is contained in the UV outer region, with no support hypothesis on the\x0aauxiliary law, the auxiliary kernel or the channel. ",
   "<p>Marton's inner bound is contained in the UV outer region, with no support hypothesis on the\x0aauxiliary law, the auxiliary kernel or the channel.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.OuterBoundTransport module doc 0", "../.././",
   "# Comparing the one- and two-auxiliary-receiver outer bounds of a broadcast channel\x0a\x0aTwo genie-aided outer bounds for the two-receiver discrete memoryless broadcast channel are\x0acompared constraint by constraint. The first bound hands a single auxiliary receiver output\x0a`J` to the genie and constrains the rate triple `(R₀, R₁, R₂)` through three systems of\x0aauxiliary variables — a plain one `(W, U, V)`, a first enhanced one `(W̃, Ũ, Ṽ)` and a second\x0aenhanced one `(Ŵ, Û, V̂)`. The second bound uses two auxiliary receiver outputs, one per\x0areceiver, and constrains the same triple through two systems.\x0a\x0aThe right-hand sides of the second bound are recorded here already specialized to the pair of\x0aauxiliary receivers `(const, X)`, where the input itself is handed to the second receiver and\x0anothing to the first. Under that choice the first system of the two-receiver bound drops out\x0aand each right-hand side collapses to a closed form in the second system alone. The\x0aspecialization is taken as a definition; deriving those closed forms from the general shape is\x0anot done here. Besides the substitution itself, some of its steps use the conditional\x0aindependence of each system from the receiver outputs given the input, which the bound builds\x0ainto its witnesses; the comparisons that need that structure again carry it as an explicit\x0ahypothesis.\x0a\x0aThe one-auxiliary-receiver bound constrains the sum rate twice over, once through the enhanced\x0asystems together with `J` and once through the plain system alone. Those two right-hand sides\x0adiffer by exactly the slack in one of the compatibility conditions the same bound imposes on its\x0awitnesses, so the first of the two constraints follows from the second and never binds.\x0a\x0aThe auxiliary receiver output can be eliminated altogether from the remaining right-hand sides.\x0aEach of them opens with a minimum of two branches, one reading `J` and one not, and the\x0acompatibility condition at the level of `W` forces the minimum onto the branch that does not; the\x0aconditions at the level of `U` and of `V` then rewrite whatever tail still reads `J` in terms of\x0athe plain system. So `J` survives only in the conditions that say which witnesses the bound\x0aadmits, not in the right-hand sides those witnesses produce.\x0a\x0aFeeding one and the same system to the plain slot and to both enhanced slots turns the\x0acompatibility conditions tying the three systems together into identities, and collapses the five\x0aright-hand sides on `R₀`, on `R₀ + R₁` and on `R₀ + R₂` onto the three that are stated through\x0athe plain system alone. With the sum-rate right-hand side, which is already stated through that\x0asystem, four of the constraints survive.\x0a\x0aTwo of the right-hand sides stated through the plain system alone, the one on `R₀ + R₁` and the\x0aone on `R₀ + R₁ + R₂`, can be weighted against each other. Reading the plain system's pair\x0a`(W, U)` as a single auxiliary variable bounds that weighting at every `t ≥ 0` by a single\x0aexpression whose first term is `I(W, U; Y)`: the chain rule at `(W, U)` absorbs the first user's\x0atail, and the sum-rate right-hand side is discarded onto the branch of its inner minimum that\x0acarries the same tail. Conditional independence of `(W, U)` from the second receiver output given\x0athe input, which the bound builds into its witnesses, then rewrites the remaining conditional\x0aterm as a difference of two mutual informations with that output.\x0a\x0aEverything below relates right-hand sides of constraints to one another. Turning such a relation\x0ainto a statement about the rate regions the two bounds cut out needs the constraints themselves,\x0aand that step is not taken here.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `singleAuxCommonBound`, `singleAuxFirstUserBound`, `singleAuxSecondUserBound` — the\x0a  right-hand sides of the one-auxiliary-receiver constraints on `R₀`, on `R₀ + R₁` and on\x0a  `R₀ + R₂` that participate in the comparison.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ` and `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ` — the companion constraints\x0a  on `R₀ + R₁` and on `R₀ + R₂`, whose leading minimum opens at the first enhanced system rather\x0a  than at the second.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBoundJFree`, `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZJFree`,\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBoundJFree`, `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZJFree` — the same four\x0a  right-hand sides written without the auxiliary receiver output.\x0a* `plainCommonBound`, `plainFirstUserBound`, `plainSecondUserBound` — the right-hand sides of the\x0a  one-auxiliary-receiver constraints on `R₀`, on `R₀ + R₁` and on `R₀ + R₂` that are stated\x0a  through the plain system alone.\x0a* `twoAuxCommonBound`, `twoAuxFirstUserBound`, `twoAuxFirstUserBoundInput`,\x0a  `twoAuxSecondUserBound`, `twoAuxSecondUserBoundInput` — the five right-hand sides of the\x0a  two-auxiliary-receiver bound at `(const, X)`. The `Input` suffix marks the two of them whose\x0a  leading term reads the channel input `X` where its partner reads a receiver output.\x0a* `enhancedSumRateBound` and `plainSumRateBound` — the two right-hand sides of the\x0a  one-auxiliary-receiver bound on `R₀ + R₁ + R₂`.\x0a* `secondUserSlack` — the slack in the upper half of the compatibility condition that ties the\x0a  second user's tail in the first enhanced system to the input.\x0a* `plainDirectionalCombination` — the weighting `(1 - t) · plainFirstUserBound +\x0a  t · plainSumRateBound` of the two plain right-hand sides that the direction `(0, 1, t)` pairs.\x0a* `plainDirectionalBound` and `plainDirectionalBoundCondFree` — the two forms of the upper bound\x0a  on that weighting, `I(W, U; Y) + t · I(X; Z | W, U)` and\x0a  `I(W, U; Y) + t · (I(X; Z) - I(W, U; Z))`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `singleAuxCommonBound_le_twoAuxCommonBound` — the `R₀` constraint transports.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBound_le_twoAuxFirstUserBound` and\x0a  `twoAuxFirstUserBound_le_twoAuxFirstUserBoundInput` — the two `R₀ + R₁` constraints.\x0a* `singleAuxSecondUserBound_le_twoAuxSecondUserBound` and\x0a  `twoAuxSecondUserBound_le_twoAuxSecondUserBoundInput` — the two `R₀ + R₂` constraints,\x0a  the first of them at the single instance `J := X` of the auxiliary receiver.\x0a* `condMutualInfoReal_sub_eq_condMutualInfoReal_prod` — the closed form\x0a  `I(Ṽ; X | W̃) - I(Ṽ; Z | W̃) = I(Ṽ; X | W̃, Z)` of the tail that the `R₀ + R₂` comparison\x0a  discards.\x0a* `enhancedSumRateBound_sub_plainSumRateBound_eq_secondUserSlack` — the two sum-rate right-hand\x0a  sides differ by `secondUserSlack`.\x0a* `plainSumRateBound_le_enhancedSumRateBound` and\x0a  `le_enhancedSumRateBound_of_le_plainSumRateBound` — the resulting order and the redundancy of\x0a  the sum-rate constraint that carries `J`.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBound_eq_jFree`, `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ_eq_jFree`,\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBound_eq_jFree` and `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ_eq_jFree` — the four\x0a  right-hand sides that read the auxiliary receiver output equal ones that do not.\x0a* `singleAuxCommonBound_diagonal_eq_plainCommonBound`,\x0a  `singleAuxFirstUserBound_diagonal_eq_plainFirstUserBound`,\x0a  `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ_diagonal_eq_plainFirstUserBound`,\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBound_diagonal_eq_plainSecondUserBound` and\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ_diagonal_eq_plainSecondUserBound` — feeding one system to all\x0a  three slots collapses five of the constraints onto the three stated through the plain system\x0a  alone, whatever the auxiliary receiver output is.\x0a* `plainDirectionalCombination_le_plainDirectionalBound` — the weighting of the two plain\x0a  right-hand sides at every `t ≥ 0` is at most `I(W, U; Y) + t · I(X; Z | W, U)`.\x0a* `plainDirectionalBound_eq_condFree` and\x0a  `plainDirectionalCombination_le_plainDirectionalBoundCondFree` — the conditional tail of that\x0a  upper bound is the difference `I(X; Z) - I(W, U; Z)` once `(W, U)` is conditionally independent\x0a  of the second receiver output given the input.\x0a\x0aNone of the five comparisons uses the compatibility conditions tying the three systems of the\x0aone-auxiliary-receiver bound together; the chain rule and the data processing inequality\x0asuffice. The sum-rate identity is the opposite case: two of those conditions enter it, together\x0awith a third that equates the two branches of the inner minimum of `plainSumRateBound`, and each\x0ais carried as an explicit hypothesis.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aThe constraints mix mutual informations additively and subtractively, so they are stated over\x0a`ℝ` rather than over `ℝ≥0∞`. Since `(⊤ : ℝ≥0∞).toReal = 0`, an infinite mutual information\x0awould silently satisfy an inequality it violates; every bridge to the `ℝ≥0∞`-valued chain rule\x0atherefore goes through an explicit finiteness fact, which the finite alphabets supply.\x0a\x0aEvery random variable is an explicit binder rather than a section variable: the alphabets are\x0afree type variables, so auto-bound implicits would let a permuted application elaborate into a\x0adifferent statement.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Comparing-the-one-and-two-auxiliary-receiver-outer-bounds-of-a-broadcast-channel\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Comparing the one- and two-auxiliary-receiver outer bounds of a broadcast channel <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Comparing-the-one-and-two-auxiliary-receiver-outer-bounds-of-a-broadcast-channel\">#</a></h1><p>Two genie-aided outer bounds for the two-receiver discrete memoryless broadcast channel are\x0acompared constraint by constraint. The first bound hands a single auxiliary receiver output\x0a<code>J</code> to the genie and constrains the rate triple <code>(R₀, R₁, R₂)</code> through three systems of\x0aauxiliary variables — a plain one <code>(W, U, V)</code>, a first enhanced one <code>(W̃, Ũ, Ṽ)</code> and a second\x0aenhanced one <code>(Ŵ, Û, V̂)</code>. The second bound uses two auxiliary receiver outputs, one per\x0areceiver, and constrains the same triple through two systems.</p><p>The right-hand sides of the second bound are recorded here already specialized to the pair of\x0aauxiliary receivers <code>(const, X)</code>, where the input itself is handed to the second receiver and\x0anothing to the first. Under that choice the first system of the two-receiver bound drops out\x0aand each right-hand side collapses to a closed form in the second system alone. The\x0aspecialization is taken as a definition; deriving those closed forms from the general shape is\x0anot done here. Besides the substitution itself, some of its steps use the conditional\x0aindependence of each system from the receiver outputs given the input, which the bound builds\x0ainto its witnesses; the comparisons that need that structure again carry it as an explicit\x0ahypothesis.</p><p>The one-auxiliary-receiver bound constrains the sum rate twice over, once through the enhanced\x0asystems together with <code>J</code> and once through the plain system alone. Those two right-hand sides\x0adiffer by exactly the slack in one of the compatibility conditions the same bound imposes on its\x0awitnesses, so the first of the two constraints follows from the second and never binds.</p><p>The auxiliary receiver output can be eliminated altogether from the remaining right-hand sides.\x0aEach of them opens with a minimum of two branches, one reading <code>J</code> and one not, and the\x0acompatibility condition at the level of <code>W</code> forces the minimum onto the branch that does not; the\x0aconditions at the level of <code>U</code> and of <code>V</code> then rewrite whatever tail still reads <code>J</code> in terms of\x0athe plain system. So <code>J</code> survives only in the conditions that say which witnesses the bound\x0aadmits, not in the right-hand sides those witnesses produce.</p><p>Feeding one and the same system to the plain slot and to both enhanced slots turns the\x0acompatibility conditions tying the three systems together into identities, and collapses the five\x0aright-hand sides on <code>R₀</code>, on <code>R₀ + R₁</code> and on <code>R₀ + R₂</code> onto the three that are stated through\x0athe plain system alone. With the sum-rate right-hand side, which is already stated through that\x0asystem, four of the constraints survive.</p><p>Two of the right-hand sides stated through the plain system alone, the one on <code>R₀ + R₁</code> and the\x0aone on <code>R₀ + R₁ + R₂</code>, can be weighted against each other. Reading the plain system's pair\x0a<code>(W, U)</code> as a single auxiliary variable bounds that weighting at every <code>t ≥ 0</code> by a single\x0aexpression whose first term is <code>I(W, U; Y)</code>: the chain rule at <code>(W, U)</code> absorbs the first user's\x0atail, and the sum-rate right-hand side is discarded onto the branch of its inner minimum that\x0acarries the same tail. Conditional independence of <code>(W, U)</code> from the second receiver output given\x0athe input, which the bound builds into its witnesses, then rewrites the remaining conditional\x0aterm as a difference of two mutual informations with that output.</p><p>Everything below relates right-hand sides of constraints to one another. Turning such a relation\x0ainto a statement about the rate regions the two bounds cut out needs the constraints themselves,\x0aand that step is not taken here.</p><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>singleAuxCommonBound</code>, <code>singleAuxFirstUserBound</code>, <code>singleAuxSecondUserBound</code> — the\x0aright-hand sides of the one-auxiliary-receiver constraints on <code>R₀</code>, on <code>R₀ + R₁</code> and on\x0a<code>R₀ + R₂</code> that participate in the comparison.</li><li><code>singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ</code> and <code>singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ</code> — the companion constraints\x0aon <code>R₀ + R₁</code> and on <code>R₀ + R₂</code>, whose leading minimum opens at the first enhanced system rather\x0athan at the second.</li><li><code>singleAuxFirstUserBoundJFree</code>, <code>singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZJFree</code>,\x0a<code>singleAuxSecondUserBoundJFree</code>, <code>singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZJFree</code> — the same four\x0aright-hand sides written without the auxiliary receiver output.</li><li><code>plainCommonBound</code>, <code>plainFirstUserBound</code>, <code>plainSecondUserBound</code> — the right-hand sides of the\x0aone-auxiliary-receiver constraints on <code>R₀</code>, on <code>R₀ + R₁</code> and on <code>R₀ + R₂</code> that are stated\x0athrough the plain system alone.</li><li><code>twoAuxCommonBound</code>, <code>twoAuxFirstUserBound</code>, <code>twoAuxFirstUserBoundInput</code>,\x0a<code>twoAuxSecondUserBound</code>, <code>twoAuxSecondUserBoundInput</code> — the five right-hand sides of the\x0atwo-auxiliary-receiver bound at <code>(const, X)</code>. The <code>Input</code> suffix marks the two of them whose\x0aleading term reads the channel input <code>X</code> where its partner reads a receiver output.</li><li><code>enhancedSumRateBound</code> and <code>plainSumRateBound</code> — the two right-hand sides of the\x0aone-auxiliary-receiver bound on <code>R₀ + R₁ + R₂</code>.</li><li><code>secondUserSlack</code> — the slack in the upper half of the compatibility condition that ties the\x0asecond user's tail in the first enhanced system to the input.</li><li><code>plainDirectionalCombination</code> — the weighting <code>(1 - t) · plainFirstUserBound + t · plainSumRateBound</code> of the two plain right-hand sides that the direction <code>(0, 1, t)</code> pairs.</li><li><code>plainDirectionalBound</code> and <code>plainDirectionalBoundCondFree</code> — the two forms of the upper bound\x0aon that weighting, <code>I(W, U; Y) + t · I(X; Z | W, U)</code> and\x0a<code>I(W, U; Y) + t · (I(X; Z) - I(W, U; Z))</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>singleAuxCommonBound_le_twoAuxCommonBound</code> — the <code>R₀</code> constraint transports.</li><li><code>singleAuxFirstUserBound_le_twoAuxFirstUserBound</code> and\x0a<code>twoAuxFirstUserBound_le_twoAuxFirstUserBoundInput</code> — the two <code>R₀ + R₁</code> constraints.</li><li><code>singleAuxSecondUserBound_le_twoAuxSecondUserBound</code> and\x0a<code>twoAuxSecondUserBound_le_twoAuxSecondUserBoundInput</code> — the two <code>R₀ + R₂</code> constraints,\x0athe first of them at the single instance <code>J := X</code> of the auxiliary receiver.</li><li><code>condMutualInfoReal_sub_eq_condMutualInfoReal_prod</code> — the closed form\x0a<code>I(Ṽ; X | W̃) - I(Ṽ; Z | W̃) = I(Ṽ; X | W̃, Z)</code> of the tail that the <code>R₀ + R₂</code> comparison\x0adiscards.</li><li><code>enhancedSumRateBound_sub_plainSumRateBound_eq_secondUserSlack</code> — the two sum-rate right-hand\x0asides differ by <code>secondUserSlack</code>.</li><li><code>plainSumRateBound_le_enhancedSumRateBound</code> and\x0a<code>le_enhancedSumRateBound_of_le_plainSumRateBound</code> — the resulting order and the redundancy of\x0athe sum-rate constraint that carries <code>J</code>.</li><li><code>singleAuxFirstUserBound_eq_jFree</code>, <code>singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ_eq_jFree</code>,\x0a<code>singleAuxSecondUserBound_eq_jFree</code> and <code>singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ_eq_jFree</code> — the four\x0aright-hand sides that read the auxiliary receiver output equal ones that do not.</li><li><code>singleAuxCommonBound_diagonal_eq_plainCommonBound</code>,\x0a<code>singleAuxFirstUserBound_diagonal_eq_plainFirstUserBound</code>,\x0a<code>singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ_diagonal_eq_plainFirstUserBound</code>,\x0a<code>singleAuxSecondUserBound_diagonal_eq_plainSecondUserBound</code> and\x0a<code>singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ_diagonal_eq_plainSecondUserBound</code> — feeding one system to all\x0athree slots collapses five of the constraints onto the three stated through the plain system\x0aalone, whatever the auxiliary receiver output is.</li><li><code>plainDirectionalCombination_le_plainDirectionalBound</code> — the weighting of the two plain\x0aright-hand sides at every <code>t ≥ 0</code> is at most <code>I(W, U; Y) + t · I(X; Z | W, U)</code>.</li><li><code>plainDirectionalBound_eq_condFree</code> and\x0a<code>plainDirectionalCombination_le_plainDirectionalBoundCondFree</code> — the conditional tail of that\x0aupper bound is the difference <code>I(X; Z) - I(W, U; Z)</code> once <code>(W, U)</code> is conditionally independent\x0aof the second receiver output given the input.</li></ul><p>None of the five comparisons uses the compatibility conditions tying the three systems of the\x0aone-auxiliary-receiver bound together; the chain rule and the data processing inequality\x0asuffice. The sum-rate identity is the opposite case: two of those conditions enter it, together\x0awith a third that equates the two branches of the inner minimum of <code>plainSumRateBound</code>, and each\x0ais carried as an explicit hypothesis.</p><h2 id=\"Implementation-notes\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Implementation notes <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Implementation-notes\">#</a></h2><p>The constraints mix mutual informations additively and subtractively, so they are stated over\x0a<code>ℝ</code> rather than over <code>ℝ≥0∞</code>. Since <code>(⊤ : ℝ≥0∞).toReal = 0</code>, an infinite mutual information\x0awould silently satisfy an inequality it violates; every bridge to the <code>ℝ≥0∞</code>-valued chain rule\x0atherefore goes through an explicit finiteness fact, which the finite alphabets supply.</p><p>Every random variable is an explicit binder rather than a section variable: the alphabets are\x0afree type variables, so auto-bound implicits would let a permuted application elaborate into a\x0adifferent statement.</p>"),
