  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.FisherInfo.DeBruijn module doc 3", "./",
   "## The de Bruijn regularity predicate ",
   "<h2 id=\"The-de-Bruijn-regularity-predicate\" class=\"markdown-heading\">The de Bruijn regularity predicate <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#The-de-Bruijn-regularity-predicate\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.FisherInfo.IsRegularDeBruijnHypV2", ".././",
   "The V2 de Bruijn regularity predicate. It carries a smooth density witness\x0a`density_t : ℝ → ℝ` for the law of `X + √t · Z`, together with regularity preconditions on\x0a`X`. The Fisher information on the right-hand side of the de Bruijn identity uses\x0a`fisherInfoOfDensity` of an explicit density witness, so the Gaussian case evaluates to\x0a`1 / v`. The de Bruijn identity itself is not a field of this predicate; it is proved\x0aseparately in `debruijnIdentityV2_holds_assembled`. ",
   "<p>The V2 de Bruijn regularity predicate. It carries a smooth density witness\x0a<code>density_t : ℝ → ℝ</code> for the law of <code>X + √t · Z</code>, together with regularity preconditions on\x0a<code>X</code>. The Fisher information on the right-hand side of the de Bruijn identity uses\x0a<code>fisherInfoOfDensity</code> of an explicit density witness, so the Gaussian case evaluates to\x0a<code>1 / v</code>. The de Bruijn identity itself is not a field of this predicate; it is proved\x0aseparately in <code>debruijnIdentityV2_holds_assembled</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.FisherInfo.convDensityAdd_hasDerivAt_self", "../.././",
   "`HasDerivAt p_t (deriv p_t x) x` for `p_t = convDensityAdd pX g_t` at every `x` (`t > 0`):\x0athe spatial first derivative of the heat-flow convolution density exists, reconstructed via the\x0aparametric-integral gateway `hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le` with the\x0adomination supplied by `kernel_x_deriv1_global_bound`.\x0a\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p><code>HasDerivAt p_t (deriv p_t x) x</code> for <code>p_t = convDensityAdd pX g_t</code> at every <code>x</code> (<code>t &gt; 0</code>):\x0athe spatial first derivative of the heat-flow convolution density exists, reconstructed via the\x0aparametric-integral gateway <code>hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le</code> with the\x0adomination supplied by <code>kernel_x_deriv1_global_bound</code>.</p><p>@audit:ok</p>"),
