   "<h2 id=\"3-Pure-asymptotic-algebra-layer\" class=\"markdown-heading\">§3. Pure asymptotic algebra layer <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#3-Pure-asymptotic-algebra-layer\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.LZ78.ConverseUDObject module doc 0", ".././",
   "# LZ78 converse UD-object\x0a\x0aThis file builds the uniquely-decodable code object underlying LZ78 and\x0aapplies Mathlib's McMillan inequality to it, obtaining a Kraft bound and an\x0aexpectation-level source-coding converse for the LZ78 per-phrase\x0a`(parent, symbol)` token code.\x0a\x0a## What this delivers\x0a\x0a* §1 — `uniquelyDecodable_of_constantLength` (general, reusable; Mathlib\x0a  has no such constructor): any set of lists all of the same positive\x0a  constant length `K` is `UniquelyDecodable`. This is the classic\x0a  block-code fact and the mathematical core here — it is exactly the\x0a  UD certificate the LZ78 token stream needs (`lz78PhraseStrings` itself is\x0a  prefix-complete and *not* UD; the encoded fixed-width token set is).\x0a\x0a* §2 introduces `boolEncode` / `finBoolCode`, a concrete `K`-bit binary code\x0a  `m ↦ (range K).map (testBit m)`. Constant length `K`; injective on\x0a  `m < 2^K` (`Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq`). `finBoolCode` encodes any `Fintype`\x0a  whose card is `≤ 2^K` as fixed-width binary, injectively.\x0a\x0a* §3 builds the LZ78 token code: for a dictionary of size `c` over alphabet `α`,\x0a  the `(c+1)·|α|` possible `(parent ∈ Fin (c+1), symbol ∈ α)` tokens encode\x0a  injectively into `K = LZ78Phrase.bitLength c |α|` bits\x0a  (`(c+1)·|α| ≤ 2^K` via `Nat.lt_pow_succ_log_self`). Its image is UD (§1),\x0a  so McMillan (`McMillanKraftBridge`) gives `kraftSum 2 (fun _ => K) ≤ 1` and\x0a  the Gibbs converse `entropyD 2 P ≤ E[L] = K` for the real LZ78 token code.\x0a  `K` is exactly the per-phrase bit cost used in\x0a  `lz78GreedyEncodingLength = c · K`\x0a  (`LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/EncodingLength.lean`).\x0a\x0a## Relation to the LZ78 block-rate converse\x0a\x0aThe §3 converse is an expectation-level bound: it instantiates\x0a`McMillanKraftBridge.entropyD_le_expectedLength_of_uniquelyDecodable` at an\x0aexplicitly constructed UD code rather than at an abstract `UniquelyDecodable`\x0ahypothesis. The LZ78 block-rate converse (Cover–Thomas Eq. 13.130,\x0a`lz78Greedy_converse_ae` in `LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/ParentBridgeConverse.lean`)\x0ais instead an a.s.-eventual, per-realization `liminf` bound. Passing from the\x0atoken-level Kraft bound to it needs the averaged ⟶ a.s. lift (Barron /\x0acompetitive optimality), which runs through the polynomial block Kraft bound\x0a`lz78_block_kraft_poly` and Borel–Cantelli, not through this file.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"LZ78-converse-UD-object\" class=\"markdown-heading\">LZ78 converse UD-object <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#LZ78-converse-UD-object\">#</a></h1><p>This file builds the uniquely-decodable code object underlying LZ78 and\x0aapplies Mathlib's McMillan inequality to it, obtaining a Kraft bound and an\x0aexpectation-level source-coding converse for the LZ78 per-phrase\x0a<code>(parent, symbol)</code> token code.</p><h2 id=\"What-this-delivers\" class=\"markdown-heading\">What this delivers <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#What-this-delivers\">#</a></h2><ul><li><p>§1 — <code>uniquelyDecodable_of_constantLength</code> (general, reusable; Mathlib\x0ahas no such constructor): any set of lists all of the same positive\x0aconstant length <code>K</code> is <code>UniquelyDecodable</code>. This is the classic\x0ablock-code fact and the mathematical core here — it is exactly the\x0aUD certificate the LZ78 token stream needs (<code>lz78PhraseStrings</code> itself is\x0aprefix-complete and <em>not</em> UD; the encoded fixed-width token set is).</p></li><li><p>§2 introduces <code>boolEncode</code> / <code>finBoolCode</code>, a concrete <code>K</code>-bit binary code\x0a<code>m ↦ (range K).map (testBit m)</code>. Constant length <code>K</code>; injective on\x0a<code>m &lt; 2^K</code> (<code>Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq</code>). <code>finBoolCode</code> encodes any <code>Fintype</code>\x0awhose card is <code>≤ 2^K</code> as fixed-width binary, injectively.</p></li><li><p>§3 builds the LZ78 token code: for a dictionary of size <code>c</code> over alphabet <code>α</code>,\x0athe <code>(c+1)·|α|</code> possible <code>(parent ∈ Fin (c+1), symbol ∈ α)</code> tokens encode\x0ainjectively into <code>K = LZ78Phrase.bitLength c |α|</code> bits\x0a(<code>(c+1)·|α| ≤ 2^K</code> via <code>Nat.lt_pow_succ_log_self</code>). Its image is UD (§1),\x0aso McMillan (<code>McMillanKraftBridge</code>) gives <code>kraftSum 2 (fun _ =&gt; K) ≤ 1</code> and\x0athe Gibbs converse <code>entropyD 2 P ≤ E[L] = K</code> for the real LZ78 token code.\x0a<code>K</code> is exactly the per-phrase bit cost used in\x0a<code>lz78GreedyEncodingLength = c · K</code>\x0a(<code><a href=\".././LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/EncodingLength.html\">LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/EncodingLength.lean</a></code>).</p></li></ul><h2 id=\"Relation-to-the-LZ78-block-rate-converse\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Relation to the LZ78 block-rate converse <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Relation-to-the-LZ78-block-rate-converse\">#</a></h2><p>The §3 converse is an expectation-level bound: it instantiates\x0a<code>McMillanKraftBridge.entropyD_le_expectedLength_of_uniquelyDecodable</code> at an\x0aexplicitly constructed UD code rather than at an abstract <code>UniquelyDecodable</code>\x0ahypothesis. The LZ78 block-rate converse (Cover–Thomas Eq. 13.130,\x0a<code>lz78Greedy_converse_ae</code> in <code><a href=\".././LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/ParentBridgeConverse.html\">LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/ParentBridgeConverse.lean</a></code>)\x0ais instead an a.s.-eventual, per-realization <code>liminf</code> bound. Passing from the\x0atoken-level Kraft bound to it needs the averaged ⟶ a.s. lift (Barron /\x0acompetitive optimality), which runs through the polynomial block Kraft bound\x0a<code>lz78_block_kraft_poly</code> and Borel–Cantelli, not through this file.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.LZ78.GreedyLongestPrefix module doc 4", "../.././",
   "### Slice / content correspondence ",
   "<h3 id=\"Slice-content-correspondence\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Slice / content correspondence <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Slice-content-correspondence\">#</a></h3>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.LZ78.GreedyParsing module doc 0", "./",
   "# LZ78 greedy parsing — per-phrase bit length\x0a\x0aThis file publishes the per-phrase bit-length cost of an LZ78\x0aphrase: the number of bits needed to encode one `(parent-index, symbol)`\x0apair given a dictionary size and an alphabet size. This is the\x0aCover–Thomas Ch.13.5 per-phrase cost form\x0a\x0a```\x0aLZ78Phrase.bitLength c a = Nat.log 2 (c + 1) + Nat.log 2 a + 2\x0a```\x0a\x0a(each phrase index needs `log(dictSize)` bits, each appended symbol\x0aneeds `log(|α|)` bits, plus a constant overhead). It is the shared\x0ainfrastructure consumed by the genuine longest-prefix greedy\x0aencoding-length development\x0a(`InformationTheory/Shannon/LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality.lean`) and by the\x0auniquely-decodable token code\x0a(`InformationTheory/Shannon/LZ78/ConverseUDObject.lean`).\x0a\x0a## File layout\x0a\x0a* §1. Per-phrase bit length — `LZ78Phrase.bitLength`: the number\x0a  of bits to encode one `(parent-index, symbol)` pair given a\x0a  dictionary size and alphabet size, together with its `simp` normal\x0a  form, monotonicity in the dictionary size, and positivity.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"LZ78-greedy-parsing-per-phrase-bit-length\" class=\"markdown-heading\">LZ78 greedy parsing — per-phrase bit length <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#LZ78-greedy-parsing-per-phrase-bit-length\">#</a></h1><p>This file publishes the per-phrase bit-length cost of an LZ78\x0aphrase: the number of bits needed to encode one <code>(parent-index, symbol)</code>\x0apair given a dictionary size and an alphabet size. This is the\x0aCover–Thomas Ch.13.5 per-phrase cost form</p><pre><code>LZ78Phrase.bitLength c a = Nat.log 2 (c + 1) + Nat.log 2 a + 2\x0a</code></pre><p>(each phrase index needs <code>log(dictSize)</code> bits, each appended symbol\x0aneeds <code>log(|α|)</code> bits, plus a constant overhead). It is the shared\x0ainfrastructure consumed by the genuine longest-prefix greedy\x0aencoding-length development\x0a(<code><a href=\"./InformationTheory/Shannon/LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality.html\">InformationTheory/Shannon/LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality.lean</a></code>) and by the\x0auniquely-decodable token code\x0a(<code><a href=\"./InformationTheory/Shannon/LZ78/ConverseUDObject.html\">InformationTheory/Shannon/LZ78/ConverseUDObject.lean</a></code>).</p><h2 id=\"File-layout\" class=\"markdown-heading\">File layout <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#File-layout\">#</a></h2><ul><li>§1. Per-phrase bit length — <code>LZ78Phrase.bitLength</code>: the number\x0aof bits to encode one <code>(parent-index, symbol)</code> pair given a\x0adictionary size and alphabet size, together with its <code>simp</code> normal\x0aform, monotonicity in the dictionary size, and positivity.</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.lz78PhraseStrings_mul_log_le_of_length", ".././",
   "The `c·log c ≤ K·n` bound for a length-`n` input family: for any\x0afamily `input : ℕ → List α` with `(input n).length = n`, the distinct\x0aphrase count `c(n) = (lz78PhraseStrings (input n)).length` satisfies the\x0aCover–Thomas product bound `(★)` with `K = 8·log(|α|+1)`. ",
   "<p>The <code>c·log c ≤ K·n</code> bound for a length-<code>n</code> input family: for any\x0afamily <code>input : ℕ → List α</code> with <code>(input n).length = n</code>, the distinct\x0aphrase count <code>c(n) = (lz78PhraseStrings (input n)).length</code> satisfies the\x0aCover–Thomas product bound <code>(★)</code> with <code>K = 8·log(|α|+1)</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.blockRV_succ_preimage_iUnion_eq", "../.././",
   "Over the symbol `a`, the extended-cylinder preimages\x0a`{blockRV (m+1) = Fin.snoc v a}` partition the `m`-block cylinder\x0a`{blockRV m = v}`. ",
   "<p>Over the symbol <code>a</code>, the extended-cylinder preimages\x0a<code>{blockRV (m+1) = Fin.snoc v a}</code> partition the <code>m</code>-block cylinder\x0a<code>{blockRV m = v}</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.LZ78.ZivEntropyBridge module doc 2", "./",
   "## Base-2 (bit) layer — unit correction for the LZ78 headline\x0a\x0aThe LZ78 encoding length `lz78GreedyEncodingLength`\x0a(`LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/EncodingLength.lean`) is measured in bits\x0a(`LZ78Phrase.bitLength` uses `Nat.log 2`, the binary code-length), whereas\x0a`blockLogAvg` / `entropyRate` are natural-log quantities (nats). The\x0aCover–Thomas Theorem 13.5.3 statement is bit-based:\x0a\x0a```\x0a(lz n x)/n → H₂   where  H₂ = (entropy rate in bits) = entropyRate / log 2.\x0a```\x0a\x0aWe therefore introduce the base-2 (bit) versions `blockLogAvg₂` and\x0a`entropyRate₂` as the natural-log quantities divided by `Real.log 2`. These\x0aare *unit conversions*, not new content: `blockLogAvg₂ = blockLogAvg / log 2`\x0aconverges to `entropyRate₂ = entropyRate / log 2` directly from SMB. The\x0aLZ78 achievability / converse bounds, being bit-based\x0a(`c·log₂c ≤ -log₂ Pₙ`), are stated against `blockLogAvg₂`. ",
   "<h2 id=\"Base-2-bit-layer-unit-correction-for-the-LZ78-headline\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Base-2 (bit) layer — unit correction for the LZ78 headline <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Base-2-bit-layer-unit-correction-for-the-LZ78-headline\">#</a></h2><p>The LZ78 encoding length <code>lz78GreedyEncodingLength</code>\x0a(<code><a href=\"./LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/EncodingLength.html\">LZ78/AsymptoticOptimality/EncodingLength.lean</a></code>) is measured in bits\x0a(<code>LZ78Phrase.bitLength</code> uses <code>Nat.log 2</code>, the binary code-length), whereas\x0a<code>blockLogAvg</code> / <code>entropyRate</code> are natural-log quantities (nats). The\x0aCover–Thomas Theorem 13.5.3 statement is bit-based:</p><pre><code>(lz n x)/n → H₂   where  H₂ = (entropy rate in bits) = entropyRate / log 2.\x0a</code></pre><p>We therefore introduce the base-2 (bit) versions <code>blockLogAvg₂</code> and\x0a<code>entropyRate₂</code> as the natural-log quantities divided by <code>Real.log 2</code>. These\x0aare <em>unit conversions</em>, not new content: <code>blockLogAvg₂ = blockLogAvg / log 2</code>\x0aconverges to <code>entropyRate₂ = entropyRate / log 2</code> directly from SMB. The\x0aLZ78 achievability / converse bounds, being bit-based\x0a(<code>c·log₂c ≤ -log₂ Pₙ</code>), are stated against <code>blockLogAvg₂</code>.</p>"),
