  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MaxEntropy.ConstrainedKKT module doc 1", ".././",
   "## Section 1 — Log-partition function and exponential-family pmf ",
   "<h2 id=\"Section-1-Log-partition-function-and-exponential-family-pmf\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Section 1 — Log-partition function and exponential-family pmf <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Section-1-Log-partition-function-and-exponential-family-pmf\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MaxEntropyConstrainedKKT.expFamilyDist_lam_zero_eq", "../.././",
   "KKT zero-multiplier reduction: `expFamilyDist f 0 = expFamilyDist g 0`\x0afor any features `f`, `g` (both equal to the uniform pmf). This is the\x0aunconstrained-Lagrangian degenerate case `λ = 0`. ",
   "<p>KKT zero-multiplier reduction: <code>expFamilyDist f 0 = expFamilyDist g 0</code>\x0afor any features <code>f</code>, <code>g</code> (both equal to the uniform pmf). This is the\x0aunconstrained-Lagrangian degenerate case <code>λ = 0</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.klDiv_prod_eq_add", "./",
   "KL divergence of product measures is additive: if both `μ₁, μ₂` are probability\x0ameasures on `α` and `ν₁, ν₂` are finite measures on `β`, then\x0a`klDiv (μ₁.prod ν₁) (μ₂.prod ν₂) = klDiv μ₁ μ₂ + klDiv ν₁ ν₂`. Derived from\x0a`klDiv_compProd_eq_add` (Mathlib) with constant kernels + `klDiv_prod_const_left`. ",
   "<p>KL divergence of product measures is additive: if both <code>μ₁, μ₂</code> are probability\x0ameasures on <code>α</code> and <code>ν₁, ν₂</code> are finite measures on <code>β</code>, then\x0a<code>klDiv (μ₁.prod ν₁) (μ₂.prod ν₂) = klDiv μ₁ μ₂ + klDiv ν₁ ν₂</code>. Derived from\x0a<code>klDiv_compProd_eq_add</code> (Mathlib) with constant kernels + <code>klDiv_prod_const_left</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.MACCodebook", ".././",
   "A length-`n` codebook for one MAC user: a function from message indices to\x0alength-`n` words.  Two codebooks (one per user) make up a `MACCode`. ",
   "<p>A length-<code>n</code> codebook for one MAC user: a function from message indices to\x0alength-<code>n</code> words.  Two codebooks (one per user) make up a <code>MACCode</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.mac_achievability", "../.././",
   "Achievability of the MAC corner-point region (Cover–Thomas Theorem 15.3.1): for an\x0aindependent product input `p₁ ⊗ p₂` with full-support marginals and a full-support MAC\x0achannel `W`, any rate pair `(R₁, R₂)` strictly inside the corner-point region\x0a`R₁ < I(X₁; (X₂, Y))`, `R₂ < I(X₂; (X₁, Y))`, `R₁ + R₂ < I((X₁, X₂); Y)` is\x0aachievable: for every target error `ε' > 0` there is `N` such that for all `n ≥ N`\x0athere is a length-`n` two-user code with at least `exp(n R₁)` / `exp(n R₂)` messages per\x0auser whose average error probability is `< ε'`.\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>Achievability of the MAC corner-point region (Cover–Thomas Theorem 15.3.1): for an\x0aindependent product input <code>p₁ ⊗ p₂</code> with full-support marginals and a full-support MAC\x0achannel <code>W</code>, any rate pair <code>(R₁, R₂)</code> strictly inside the corner-point region\x0a<code>R₁ &lt; I(X₁; (X₂, Y))</code>, <code>R₂ &lt; I(X₂; (X₁, Y))</code>, <code>R₁ + R₂ &lt; I((X₁, X₂); Y)</code> is\x0aachievable: for every target error <code>ε' &gt; 0</code> there is <code>N</code> such that for all <code>n ≥ N</code>\x0athere is a length-<code>n</code> two-user code with at least <code>exp(n R₁)</code> / <code>exp(n R₂)</code> messages per\x0auser whose average error probability is <code>&lt; ε'</code>.\x0a@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.MACCode.decoder", "./",
   "Joint pair decoder. ",
   "<p>Joint pair decoder.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.macX2s", ".././",
   "The second input coordinate `ω ↦ (ω i).2.1`. ",
   "<p>The second input coordinate <code>ω ↦ (ω i).2.1</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.macJoint_mutualInfo_eq_condMutualInfo₁", "../.././",
   "Chain rule under input independence (user 1):\x0a`I(X₁; (X₂, Y)) = I(X₁; Y | X₂)`. ",
   "<p>Chain rule under input independence (user 1):\x0a<code>I(X₁; (X₂, Y)) = I(X₁; Y | X₂)</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.macConcatCode", "./",
   "Block concatenation of a length-`n₁` code (user counts `Ka₁, Ka₂`) and a length-`n₂`\x0acode (user counts `Kb₁, Kb₂`) into a length-`n₁ + n₂` code with user counts\x0a`Ka₁·Kb₁, Ka₂·Kb₂`.  User messages are paired via `finProdFinEquiv`, codewords are\x0aconcatenated with `Fin.append`, and the joint decoder splits the received block at `n₁` and\x0adecodes each half independently. ",
   "<p>Block concatenation of a length-<code>n₁</code> code (user counts <code>Ka₁, Ka₂</code>) and a length-<code>n₂</code>\x0acode (user counts <code>Kb₁, Kb₂</code>) into a length-<code>n₁ + n₂</code> code with user counts\x0a<code>Ka₁·Kb₁, Ka₂·Kb₂</code>.  User messages are paired via <code>finProdFinEquiv</code>, codewords are\x0aconcatenated with <code>Fin.append</code>, and the joint decoder splits the received block at <code>n₁</code> and\x0adecodes each half independently.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MAC.macConverse_isMarkovChain", ".././",
   "Markov chain `(messages) → (encoded inputs) → (outputs)` for the constructed ambient.\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>Markov chain <code>(messages) → (encoded inputs) → (outputs)</code> for the constructed ambient.\x0a@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MultivariateDiffEntropy module doc 6", "../.././",
   "## 2-variable Bayes density split\x0a\x0aThe family below supplies the `h_llr_split` hypothesis of\x0a`klDiv_prod_marginals_toReal_eq_sum_sub_joint_of_llr_split` and\x0a`jointDifferentialEntropy_le_sum_of_llr_split` internally, via Mathlib's\x0a`prod_withDensity` + `rnDeriv_mul_rnDeriv`. ",
   "<h2 id=\"2-variable-Bayes-density-split\" class=\"markdown-heading\">2-variable Bayes density split <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#2-variable-Bayes-density-split\">#</a></h2><p>The family below supplies the <code>h_llr_split</code> hypothesis of\x0a<code>klDiv_prod_marginals_toReal_eq_sum_sub_joint_of_llr_split</code> and\x0a<code>jointDifferentialEntropy_le_sum_of_llr_split</code> internally, via Mathlib's\x0a<code>prod_withDensity</code> + <code>rnDeriv_mul_rnDeriv</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MutualInfoReencoding module doc 0", "./",
   "# Invariance of an information slot under a re-encoding of one of its variables\x0a\x0aAn information slot depends on the variable filling it only through the information that variable\x0acarries, so replacing the variable by its image under a map that has a left inverse leaves the slot\x0aunchanged: the data processing inequality bounds the slot in one direction along the map and in the\x0aother along the left inverse.  The same holds for a variable replaced by one equal to it almost\x0aeverywhere, since the two induce the same joint law.  For a re-encoding of the conditioning\x0avariable the chain rule turns the statement about a pair back into one about the conditional\x0amutual information.\x0a\x0aNone of the statements mentions a channel or a code: they are properties of `mutualInfo` and\x0a`condMutualInfo` under a substitution of one of their variables.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse` — re-encoding a variable by a map that has a left inverse leaves\x0a  the mutual information it carries about another variable unchanged.\x0a* `mutualInfo_congr_ae` — two variables that agree almost everywhere carry the same mutual\x0a  information about a third.\x0a* `condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond` — the same re-encoding applied to the conditioning\x0a  variable of a conditional mutual information, obtained from the chain rule by cancelling the\x0a  tag term on both sides.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aA variable relabeled by a `MeasurableEquiv` is the special case `g := e.symm`, so\x0a`mutualInfo_map_left_measurableEquiv` is an instance of `mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse`; it is\x0astated separately because it is proved directly from `klDiv_map_measurableEquiv`, without the\x0adata processing inequality.  Its conditioner analogue `condMutualInfo_map_cond_measurableEquiv`\x0ais not subsumed the same way: cancelling the conditioning variable's own term of the chain rule,\x0awhich is how `condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond` is proved, needs that term to be finite,\x0awhereas a relabel needs no side condition at all.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Invariance-of-an-information-slot-under-a-re-encoding-of-one-of-its-variables\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Invariance of an information slot under a re-encoding of one of its variables <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Invariance-of-an-information-slot-under-a-re-encoding-of-one-of-its-variables\">#</a></h1><p>An information slot depends on the variable filling it only through the information that variable\x0acarries, so replacing the variable by its image under a map that has a left inverse leaves the slot\x0aunchanged: the data processing inequality bounds the slot in one direction along the map and in the\x0aother along the left inverse.  The same holds for a variable replaced by one equal to it almost\x0aeverywhere, since the two induce the same joint law.  For a re-encoding of the conditioning\x0avariable the chain rule turns the statement about a pair back into one about the conditional\x0amutual information.</p><p>None of the statements mentions a channel or a code: they are properties of <code>mutualInfo</code> and\x0a<code>condMutualInfo</code> under a substitution of one of their variables.</p><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse</code> — re-encoding a variable by a map that has a left inverse leaves\x0athe mutual information it carries about another variable unchanged.</li><li><code>mutualInfo_congr_ae</code> — two variables that agree almost everywhere carry the same mutual\x0ainformation about a third.</li><li><code>condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond</code> — the same re-encoding applied to the conditioning\x0avariable of a conditional mutual information, obtained from the chain rule by cancelling the\x0atag term on both sides.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Implementation-notes\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Implementation notes <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Implementation-notes\">#</a></h2><p>A variable relabeled by a <code>MeasurableEquiv</code> is the special case <code>g := e.symm</code>, so\x0a<code>mutualInfo_map_left_measurableEquiv</code> is an instance of <code>mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse</code>; it is\x0astated separately because it is proved directly from <code>klDiv_map_measurableEquiv</code>, without the\x0adata processing inequality.  Its conditioner analogue <code>condMutualInfo_map_cond_measurableEquiv</code>\x0ais not subsumed the same way: cancelling the conditioning variable's own term of the chain rule,\x0awhich is how <code>condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond</code> is proved, needs that term to be finite,\x0awhereas a relabel needs no side condition at all.</p>"),
