  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ShannonHartley.Converse module doc 2", ".././",
   "## Constant-noise parallel channel and its measurability discharge ",
   "<h2 id=\"Constant-noise-parallel-channel-and-its-measurability-discharge\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Constant-noise parallel channel and its measurability discharge <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Constant-noise-parallel-channel-and-its-measurability-discharge\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ShannonHartley.ConverseCount module doc 0", "../.././",
   "# Count domination for the Shannon–Hartley converse\x0a\x0aFor an arbitrary code's orthonormal, time-limited test family `φ : Fin k → E` (with `E =\x0aL²(ℝ;ℂ)`), the number of *band-limited Gram* eigenvalues exceeding `c` is at most\x0a`prolateCount T W c`. This is the bridge that dominates an arbitrary code's Gram spectrum by the\x0aoperator spectrum of `A = timeBandLimitingOp T W`; it feeds the water-filling head-count.\x0a\x0aThe band-limited Gram matrix is `Gᵢⱼ = ⟪P_W φᵢ, P_W φⱼ⟫`, whose eigenvalues are counted by\x0a`bandGramEigenvalues`. The proof realizes the high-eigenvalue eigenspace inside `E` (via the\x0aeigenvector images `gramEig`), where the operator-side Bessel domination\x0a`frame_form_le_op_form` supplies the Rayleigh bound feeding\x0a`finrank_le_prolateCount_of_form_gt` (the abstract min-max half of Cauchy interlacing).\x0a\x0aIt is non-circular: it does not assume codewords = prolate basis.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Count-domination-for-the-Shannon-Hartley-converse\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Count domination for the Shannon–Hartley converse <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Count-domination-for-the-Shannon-Hartley-converse\">#</a></h1><p>For an arbitrary code's orthonormal, time-limited test family <code>φ : Fin k → E</code> (with <code>E = L²(ℝ;ℂ)</code>), the number of <em>band-limited Gram</em> eigenvalues exceeding <code>c</code> is at most\x0a<code>prolateCount T W c</code>. This is the bridge that dominates an arbitrary code's Gram spectrum by the\x0aoperator spectrum of <code>A = timeBandLimitingOp T W</code>; it feeds the water-filling head-count.</p><p>The band-limited Gram matrix is <code>Gᵢⱼ = ⟪P_W φᵢ, P_W φⱼ⟫</code>, whose eigenvalues are counted by\x0a<code>bandGramEigenvalues</code>. The proof realizes the high-eigenvalue eigenspace inside <code>E</code> (via the\x0aeigenvector images <code>gramEig</code>), where the operator-side Bessel domination\x0a<code>frame_form_le_op_form</code> supplies the Rayleigh bound feeding\x0a<code>finrank_le_prolateCount_of_form_gt</code> (the abstract min-max half of Cauchy interlacing).</p><p>It is non-circular: it does not assume codewords = prolate basis.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ShannonHartley.Operational module doc 1", "./",
   "## §A — Band-limited signals ",
   "<h2 id=\"A-Band-limited-signals\" class=\"markdown-heading\">§A — Band-limited signals <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#A-Band-limited-signals\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ShannonHartley.Preequalizer module doc 0", ".././",
   "# Pre-equalizer: bounded-below endomorphisms are invertible with norm control\x0a\x0aThe geometric core of the Shannon–Hartley achievability route (\"route (ii)\", the operator lower\x0abound). A linear endomorphism `A` of a finite-dimensional inner-product space that is bounded below,\x0a`c ‖v‖² ≤ ‖A v‖²` with `c > 0`, is surjective, and every target `t` has a preimage `a` whose energy\x0ais controlled: `‖a‖² ≤ (1/c) ‖t‖²`. This replaces the matrix-inverse `G⁻¹` step of the informal\x0asketch with a self-contained finite-dimensional fact.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Pre-equalizer-bounded-below-endomorphisms-are-invertible-with-norm-control\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Pre-equalizer: bounded-below endomorphisms are invertible with norm control <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Pre-equalizer-bounded-below-endomorphisms-are-invertible-with-norm-control\">#</a></h1><p>The geometric core of the Shannon–Hartley achievability route (&quot;route (ii)&quot;, the operator lower\x0abound). A linear endomorphism <code>A</code> of a finite-dimensional inner-product space that is bounded below,\x0a<code>c ‖v‖² ≤ ‖A v‖²</code> with <code>c &gt; 0</code>, is surjective, and every target <code>t</code> has a preimage <code>a</code> whose energy\x0ais controlled: <code>‖a‖² ≤ (1/c) ‖t‖²</code>. This replaces the matrix-inverse <code>G⁻¹</code> step of the informal\x0asketch with a self-contained finite-dimensional fact.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ShannonHartley.waterfill_head_tail_bound", "../.././",
   "The fixed-`T` water-filling head/tail split. Splitting the slots into \"loud\" ones\x0a(`c₀ < νᵢ`, at most `B` of them by the count hypothesis) and \"quiet\" ones, the total per-slot\x0alog-capacity is bounded by a tail term `c₀·TP/N₀` (linearization `log(1+x) ≤ x` on the quiet slots)\x0aplus a head term `B · ½ log(1 + TP/(B·N₀/2))` (Jensen + monotonicity on the loud slots). ",
   "<p>The fixed-<code>T</code> water-filling head/tail split. Splitting the slots into &quot;loud&quot; ones\x0a(<code>c₀ &lt; νᵢ</code>, at most <code>B</code> of them by the count hypothesis) and &quot;quiet&quot; ones, the total per-slot\x0alog-capacity is bounded by a tail term <code>c₀·TP/N₀</code> (linearization <code>log(1+x) ≤ x</code> on the quiet slots)\x0aplus a head term <code>B · ½ log(1 + TP/(B·N₀/2))</code> (Jensen + monotonicity on the loud slots).</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.SlepianWolf.ConditionalTypicalSlice module doc 0", "./",
   "# Slepian–Wolf conditional typical slice\x0a\x0aThis file publishes the conditional typical slice size bound, the key new\x0aingredient for the full Slepian–Wolf rate region (Cover–Thomas Theorem 15.4.1).\x0aFor a fixed `Y`-block `y : Fin n → β`, the `X`-fiber of the jointly typical set\x0ais bounded in size by `exp(n · (H(X|Y) + 2ε))`, where `H(X|Y) := H(X, Y) - H(Y)`.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `conditionalTypicalSlice μ Xs Ys n ε y` —\x0a  the fiber `{x : Fin n → α | (x, y) ∈ jointlyTypicalSet μ Xs Ys n ε}`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `conditionalTypicalSlice_card_le` — the slice size is bounded by\x0a  `exp(n · (H(X, Y) - H(Y) + 2ε))`.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0a* Each fiber element `x` makes `(x, y)` jointly typical, so the joint sequence\x0a  `i ↦ (x i, y i)` lies in `typicalSet μ (jointSequence Xs Ys) n ε` and each\x0a  sample has probability at least `exp(-n · (H(X, Y) + ε))` by\x0a  `typicalSet_prob_ge`. Summed over the fiber this is at most `Pr[Yⁿ = y]`, which\x0a  for `Y`-typical `y` is at most `exp(-n · (H(Y) - ε))` by `typicalSet_prob_le`.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Slepian-Wolf-conditional-typical-slice\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Slepian–Wolf conditional typical slice <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Slepian-Wolf-conditional-typical-slice\">#</a></h1><p>This file publishes the conditional typical slice size bound, the key new\x0aingredient for the full Slepian–Wolf rate region (Cover–Thomas Theorem 15.4.1).\x0aFor a fixed <code>Y</code>-block <code>y : Fin n → β</code>, the <code>X</code>-fiber of the jointly typical set\x0ais bounded in size by <code>exp(n · (H(X|Y) + 2ε))</code>, where <code>H(X|Y) := H(X, Y) - H(Y)</code>.</p><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>conditionalTypicalSlice μ Xs Ys n ε y</code> —\x0athe fiber <code>{x : Fin n → α | (x, y) ∈ jointlyTypicalSet μ Xs Ys n ε}</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>conditionalTypicalSlice_card_le</code> — the slice size is bounded by\x0a<code>exp(n · (H(X, Y) - H(Y) + 2ε))</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Implementation-notes\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Implementation notes <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Implementation-notes\">#</a></h2><ul><li>Each fiber element <code>x</code> makes <code>(x, y)</code> jointly typical, so the joint sequence\x0a<code>i ↦ (x i, y i)</code> lies in <code>typicalSet μ (jointSequence Xs Ys) n ε</code> and each\x0asample has probability at least <code>exp(-n · (H(X, Y) + ε))</code> by\x0a<code>typicalSet_prob_ge</code>. Summed over the fiber this is at most <code>Pr[Yⁿ = y]</code>, which\x0afor <code>Y</code>-typical <code>y</code> is at most <code>exp(-n · (H(Y) - ε))</code> by <code>typicalSet_prob_le</code>.</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ChannelCoding.swError_E0", ".././",
   "`E_0`: the true source pair is not jointly typical. ",
   "<p><code>E_0</code>: the true source pair is not jointly typical.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.shannon_mcmillan_breiman", "../.././",
   "**Shannon–McMillan–Breiman theorem**: for a stationary ergodic process with\x0afinite alphabet `α`, the per-symbol negative log-likelihood `blockLogAvg μ p n`\x0aconverges almost surely to the entropy rate `entropyRate μ p`.\x0a\x0aThis is the hypothesis-free capstone: the four hypotheses of\x0a`shannon_mcmillan_breiman_of_sandwich` hold unconditionally for an ergodic\x0aprocess.\x0a\x0aSee also `algoet_cover_liminf_bound`, `algoet_cover_limsup_bound`,\x0a`blockLogAvg_bddAbove_ae`, `blockLogAvg_bddBelow_ae`. ",
   "<p><strong>Shannon–McMillan–Breiman theorem</strong>: for a stationary ergodic process with\x0afinite alphabet <code>α</code>, the per-symbol negative log-likelihood <code>blockLogAvg μ p n</code>\x0aconverges almost surely to the entropy rate <code>entropyRate μ p</code>.</p><p>This is the hypothesis-free capstone: the four hypotheses of\x0a<code>shannon_mcmillan_breiman_of_sandwich</code> hold unconditionally for an ergodic\x0aprocess.</p><p>See also <code>algoet_cover_liminf_bound</code>, <code>algoet_cover_limsup_bound</code>,\x0a<code>blockLogAvg_bddAbove_ae</code>, <code>blockLogAvg_bddBelow_ae</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MRatioUp_le_sq_eventually", "./",
   "Borel–Cantelli consequence: the upward ratio is eventually bounded by `n²` a.s. ",
   "<p>Borel–Cantelli consequence: the upward ratio is eventually bounded by <code>n²</code> a.s.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.SMB.ChainRule module doc 2", ".././",
   "## Multiplicative chain rule at singletons (ENNReal level) ",
   "<h2 id=\"Multiplicative-chain-rule-at-singletons-ENNReal-level\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Multiplicative chain rule at singletons (ENNReal level) <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Multiplicative-chain-rule-at-singletons-ENNReal-level\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.FisherInfo.stam_convex_fisher_bound_gaussian", "../.././",
   "The Gaussian **Stam convex Fisher bound**. For Gaussian laws `𝒩(m₁,v₁)`, `𝒩(m₂,v₂)`\x0awith `v₁, v₂ ≠ 0`, the V2 Fisher information of the sum law `𝒩(m₁+m₂, v₁+v₂)`\x0asatisfies `J(X+Y) ≤ λ² J(X) + (1-λ)² J(Y)` for every `λ ∈ [0,1]`.\x0aEach Fisher info evaluates to the closed form `1/v` via `fisherInfoOfMeasureV2_gaussianReal`. ",
   "<p>The Gaussian <strong>Stam convex Fisher bound</strong>. For Gaussian laws <code>𝒩(m₁,v₁)</code>, <code>𝒩(m₂,v₂)</code>\x0awith <code>v₁, v₂ ≠ 0</code>, the V2 Fisher information of the sum law <code>𝒩(m₁+m₂, v₁+v₂)</code>\x0asatisfies <code>J(X+Y) ≤ λ² J(X) + (1-λ)² J(Y)</code> for every <code>λ ∈ [0,1]</code>.\x0aEach Fisher info evaluates to the closed form <code>1/v</code> via <code>fisherInfoOfMeasureV2_gaussianReal</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.Stein.Achievability module doc 0", "./",
   "# Stein's lemma: achievability\x0a\x0aStein's lemma for binary hypothesis testing (Cover–Thomas, Theorem 11.8.3): the optimal\x0atype-II error of an `n`-sample test with type-I error at most `ε` decays exponentially in `n`\x0aat the rate of the Kullback–Leibler divergence `klDiv P Q`. This file develops the achievability\x0a(lower bound) side: the log-likelihood-ratio plumbing, the Stein-typical set, and the\x0atensorization of the KL divergence.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `llrPmf P Q` — the alphabet-side log-likelihood ratio `log P{x} − log Q{x}`.\x0a* `logLikelihoodRatio P Q Xs i` — the per-sample log-likelihood ratio along a process `Xs`.\x0a* `steinTypicalSet P Q n ε` — blocks whose empirical log-likelihood ratio is within `ε` of\x0a  `klDiv P Q`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `stein_strong_law` — the empirical mean of the log-likelihood ratio converges almost surely\x0a  to `(klDiv P Q).toReal`.\x0a* `stein_inProbability` — the same convergence holds in probability.\x0a* `stein_achievability` — there exist `ε`-level tests whose type-II error decays at rate\x0a  `klDiv P Q`.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0a* The log-likelihood-ratio plumbing is obtained as the two-distribution specialization of the\x0a  AEP development, which lets most measurability, identical-distribution, and independence\x0a  lemmas be reused rather than reproved.\x0a* Over a finite alphabet `α` with `0 < Q.real {x}` for every `x`, all quantities are expanded\x0a  point-wise, avoiding the general Radon–Nikodym derivative machinery for product measures.\x0a\x0a## References\x0a\x0a* T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, *Elements of Information Theory* (2nd ed.),\x0a  Wiley, 2006. Theorem 11.8.3.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Stein-s-lemma-achievability\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Stein's lemma: achievability <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Stein-s-lemma-achievability\">#</a></h1><p>Stein's lemma for binary hypothesis testing (Cover–Thomas, Theorem 11.8.3): the optimal\x0atype-II error of an <code>n</code>-sample test with type-I error at most <code>ε</code> decays exponentially in <code>n</code>\x0aat the rate of the Kullback–Leibler divergence <code>klDiv P Q</code>. This file develops the achievability\x0a(lower bound) side: the log-likelihood-ratio plumbing, the Stein-typical set, and the\x0atensorization of the KL divergence.</p><h2 id=\"Main-definitions\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main definitions <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-definitions\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>llrPmf P Q</code> — the alphabet-side log-likelihood ratio <code>log P{x} − log Q{x}</code>.</li><li><code>logLikelihoodRatio P Q Xs i</code> — the per-sample log-likelihood ratio along a process <code>Xs</code>.</li><li><code>steinTypicalSet P Q n ε</code> — blocks whose empirical log-likelihood ratio is within <code>ε</code> of\x0a<code>klDiv P Q</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Main-statements\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Main statements <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Main-statements\">#</a></h2><ul><li><code>stein_strong_law</code> — the empirical mean of the log-likelihood ratio converges almost surely\x0ato <code>(klDiv P Q).toReal</code>.</li><li><code>stein_inProbability</code> — the same convergence holds in probability.</li><li><code>stein_achievability</code> — there exist <code>ε</code>-level tests whose type-II error decays at rate\x0a<code>klDiv P Q</code>.</li></ul><h2 id=\"Implementation-notes\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Implementation notes <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Implementation-notes\">#</a></h2><ul><li>The log-likelihood-ratio plumbing is obtained as the two-distribution specialization of the\x0aAEP development, which lets most measurability, identical-distribution, and independence\x0alemmas be reused rather than reproved.</li><li>Over a finite alphabet <code>α</code> with <code>0 &lt; Q.real {x}</code> for every <code>x</code>, all quantities are expanded\x0apoint-wise, avoiding the general Radon–Nikodym derivative machinery for product measures.</li></ul><h2 id=\"References\" class=\"markdown-heading\">References <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#References\">#</a></h2><ul><li>T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, <em>Elements of Information Theory</em> (2nd ed.),\x0aWiley, 2006. Theorem 11.8.3.</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.steinOptimalBeta_log_ge_of_achievability", ".././",
   "Achievability-side lower bound on the type-II exponent: eventually\x0a`(klDiv P Q).toReal − δ ≤ -(1/n) log (steinOptimalBeta P Q n ε)`. ",
   "<p>Achievability-side lower bound on the type-II exponent: eventually\x0a<code>(klDiv P Q).toReal − δ ≤ -(1/n) log (steinOptimalBeta P Q n ε)</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.IsSufficientStatistic", "../.././",
   "Sufficient statistic in markov-chain form: the chain `Xs → f∘Xs → θ` is a Markov chain,\x0ai.e., the statistic `T(X) = f(X)` separates `Xs` and `θ` (conditional independence `Xs ⊥ θ ∣ T(X)`).\x0a\x0aEquivalence with the Neyman-Fisher factorization form is proved in `isSufficient_iff_factorized`.\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>Sufficient statistic in markov-chain form: the chain <code>Xs → f∘Xs → θ</code> is a Markov chain,\x0ai.e., the statistic <code>T(X) = f(X)</code> separates <code>Xs</code> and <code>θ</code> (conditional independence <code>Xs ⊥ θ ∣ T(X)</code>).</p><p>Equivalence with the Neyman-Fisher factorization form is proved in <code>isSufficient_iff_factorized</code>.\x0a@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TimeBandLimiting.Count module doc 1", "./",
   "### Operator-level bricks for the achievability pre-equalizer\x0a\x0aThe continuous-time AWGN achievability receiver sees a band-limited codeword `v ∈ V =\x0a`prolateEigenspaceSup T W c`` through the time-limiting filter `Q_T`. The core operator fact is the\x0a*time-window energy concentration*: on `V` the time-limited energy `‖Q_T v‖²` retains at least the\x0afraction `c` of the total energy `‖v‖²`. These three bricks package that into the exact shapes the\x0apre-equalizer consumes: the concentration inequality itself, the injectivity of `Q_T|_V` it implies,\x0aand the Gram lower bound `G ≥ c·I` on a `V`-ONB used to bound the pre-equalizer gain `G⁻¹ ≤ (1/c)I`.\x0a\x0aThe dominant cost of the eventual `testFn` construction is the `Lp`-class → pointwise `ℝ → ℝ`\x0arepresentative lift (route-independent); the `testFn` themselves are the `[0,T]`-supported real\x0aONB of `Q_T(V)`.\x0a",
   "<h3 id=\"Operator-level-bricks-for-the-achievability-pre-equalizer\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Operator-level bricks for the achievability pre-equalizer <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Operator-level-bricks-for-the-achievability-pre-equalizer\">#</a></h3><p>The continuous-time AWGN achievability receiver sees a band-limited codeword <code>v ∈ V = </code>prolateEigenspaceSup T W c`` through the time-limiting filter <code>Q_T</code>. The core operator fact is the\x0a<em>time-window energy concentration</em>: on <code>V</code> the time-limited energy <code>‖Q_T v‖²</code> retains at least the\x0afraction <code>c</code> of the total energy <code>‖v‖²</code>. These three bricks package that into the exact shapes the\x0apre-equalizer consumes: the concentration inequality itself, the injectivity of <code>Q_T|_V</code> it implies,\x0aand the Gram lower bound <code>G ≥ c·I</code> on a <code>V</code>-ONB used to bound the pre-equalizer gain <code>G⁻¹ ≤ (1/c)I</code>.</p><p>The dominant cost of the eventual <code>testFn</code> construction is the <code>Lp</code>-class → pointwise <code>ℝ → ℝ</code>\x0arepresentative lift (route-independent); the <code>testFn</code> themselves are the <code>[0,T]</code>-supported real\x0aONB of <code>Q_T(V)</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TimeBandLimiting.Enumeration module doc 3", ".././",
   "### The `2WT` trace bound\x0a\x0aThe crude `2WT` trace bound — the part of the degrees-of-freedom story that Bessel reaches on its\x0aown. (The Landau–Pollak–Slepian *concentration* is a strictly stronger statement and is not proved\x0ahere; see `prolateCount_mul_le`.) The band-limiting projection\x0ais an integral operator against the reproducing kernel `k_t = 2W sincN(2W(t − ·))`\x0a(`bandLimitProj_apply_ae`), so `(P_W f)(t) = ⟪k_t, f⟫`. Two facts about that kernel drive everything\x0ahere: its `L²`-norm is the constant `‖k_t‖² = 2W` (Plancherel against the spectral boxcar, which is\x0aalready in-tree), and the quadratic form of `A` reads `⟪A f, f⟫ = ∫_[0,T] |⟪k_t, f⟫|² dt`.\x0a\x0aBessel's inequality applied pointwise in `t` then caps the trace of `A` along any finite orthonormal\x0afamily by `∫_[0,T] ‖k_t‖² dt = 2WT`, and Markov's inequality converts that into the eigenvalue\x0acounting bound `c · #{λ > c} ≤ 2WT`.\x0a",
   "<h3 id=\"The-2WT-trace-bound\" class=\"markdown-heading\">The <code>2WT</code> trace bound <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#The-2WT-trace-bound\">#</a></h3><p>The crude <code>2WT</code> trace bound — the part of the degrees-of-freedom story that Bessel reaches on its\x0aown. (The Landau–Pollak–Slepian <em>concentration</em> is a strictly stronger statement and is not proved\x0ahere; see <code>prolateCount_mul_le</code>.) The band-limiting projection\x0ais an integral operator against the reproducing kernel <code>k_t = 2W sincN(2W(t − ·))</code>\x0a(<code>bandLimitProj_apply_ae</code>), so <code>(P_W f)(t) = ⟪k_t, f⟫</code>. Two facts about that kernel drive everything\x0ahere: its <code>L²</code>-norm is the constant <code>‖k_t‖² = 2W</code> (Plancherel against the spectral boxcar, which is\x0aalready in-tree), and the quadratic form of <code>A</code> reads <code>⟪A f, f⟫ = ∫_[0,T] |⟪k_t, f⟫|² dt</code>.</p><p>Bessel's inequality applied pointwise in <code>t</code> then caps the trace of <code>A</code> along any finite orthonormal\x0afamily by <code>∫_[0,T] ‖k_t‖² dt = 2WT</code>, and Markov's inequality converts that into the eigenvalue\x0acounting bound <code>c · #{λ &gt; c} ≤ 2WT</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TimeBandLimiting.starₗE", "../.././",
   "Complex conjugation on `L²(ℝ;ℂ)` as a conjugate-linear map. Mathlib equips `Lp` with a bare\x0a`Star` instance only (no `StarAddMonoid` / `StarModule`), so the additivity and\x0aconjugate-homogeneity that bundle it into a semilinear map are supplied here by `star_add_Lp` /\x0a`star_smul_Lp`. ",
   "<p>Complex conjugation on <code>L²(ℝ;ℂ)</code> as a conjugate-linear map. Mathlib equips <code>Lp</code> with a bare\x0a<code>Star</code> instance only (no <code>StarAddMonoid</code> / <code>StarModule</code>), so the additivity and\x0aconjugate-homogeneity that bundle it into a semilinear map are supplied here by <code>star_add_Lp</code> /\x0a<code>star_smul_Lp</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TimeBandLimiting.kernelBox", "./",
   "The exhausting family of squares `[-R,R] × [-R,R]` in `ℝ × ℝ`. Each is a rectangle of finite\x0ameasure, and they increase to the whole plane; intersecting with them reduces the kernel density\x0aargument to sets of finite measure. ",
   "<p>The exhausting family of squares <code>[-R,R] × [-R,R]</code> in <code>ℝ × ℝ</code>. Each is a rectangle of finite\x0ameasure, and they increase to the whole plane; intersecting with them reduces the kernel density\x0aargument to sets of finite measure.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TimeBandLimiting.exists_eigen_hilbertBasis", ".././",
   "A complete orthonormal eigenbasis of `A = timeBandLimitingOp T W` exists. The operator is\x0acompact and self-adjoint, so its eigenspaces are total\x0a(`orthogonalComplement_iSup_eigenspaces_eq_bot`).\x0aCollating a Hilbert basis of each eigenspace (`exists_hilbertBasis`, uniform in `μ`) over\x0a`Σ μ : ℂ, …` and gluing by `mkOfOrthogonalEqBot` yields a complete orthonormal eigenbasis of `E`,\x0awith real nonnegative eigenvalues, whose vectors with eigenvalue above `c` span the high eigenspace\x0a`prolateEigenspaceSup T W c`.\x0a\x0aNo hypotheses; the whole construction is in the\x0abody. All three conjuncts are proven — the eigen-relation and nonnegativity pointwise, and\x0athe span identity by a real two-sided `le_antisymm` (not `:True`). The third conjunct is\x0anon-vacuous: were `{i | c < lam i}` empty for all `c > 0`, nonnegativity would force `lam ≡ 0`,\x0acontradicting\x0a`tsum_eigen_eq_two_mul`'s `∑ lam = 2WT ≠ 0` for `0 < W`, `0 < T`. sorryAx-free (`#print axioms` =\x0a`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`).\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>A complete orthonormal eigenbasis of <code>A = timeBandLimitingOp T W</code> exists. The operator is\x0acompact and self-adjoint, so its eigenspaces are total\x0a(<code>orthogonalComplement_iSup_eigenspaces_eq_bot</code>).\x0aCollating a Hilbert basis of each eigenspace (<code>exists_hilbertBasis</code>, uniform in <code>μ</code>) over\x0a<code>Σ μ : ℂ, …</code> and gluing by <code>mkOfOrthogonalEqBot</code> yields a complete orthonormal eigenbasis of <code>E</code>,\x0awith real nonnegative eigenvalues, whose vectors with eigenvalue above <code>c</code> span the high eigenspace\x0a<code>prolateEigenspaceSup T W c</code>.</p><p>No hypotheses; the whole construction is in the\x0abody. All three conjuncts are proven — the eigen-relation and nonnegativity pointwise, and\x0athe span identity by a real two-sided <code>le_antisymm</code> (not <code>:True</code>). The third conjunct is\x0anon-vacuous: were <code>{i | c &lt; lam i}</code> empty for all <code>c &gt; 0</code>, nonnegativity would force <code>lam ≡ 0</code>,\x0acontradicting\x0a<code>tsum_eigen_eq_two_mul</code>'s <code>∑ lam = 2WT ≠ 0</code> for <code>0 &lt; W</code>, <code>0 &lt; T</code>. sorryAx-free (<code>#print axioms</code> =\x0a<code>[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]</code>).\x0a@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.TypedRV module doc 6", "../.././",
   "## Typed-form main lemmas\x0a\x0aOne-line aliases of the measure-form lemmas. The `_rv` suffix avoids name conflicts\x0awith the bare names. ",
   "<h2 id=\"Typed-form-main-lemmas\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Typed-form main lemmas <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Typed-form-main-lemmas\">#</a></h2><p>One-line aliases of the measure-form lemmas. The <code>_rv</code> suffix avoids name conflicts\x0awith the bare names.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.WynerZiv.Achievability.Concentration module doc 0", "./",
   "# Wyner–Ziv achievability — inner concentration sub-lemmas for the Markov-lemma covering bound\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Wyner-Ziv-achievability-inner-concentration-sub-lemmas-for-the-Markov-lemma-covering-bound\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Wyner–Ziv achievability — inner concentration sub-lemmas for the Markov-lemma covering bound <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Wyner-Ziv-achievability-inner-concentration-sub-lemmas-for-the-Markov-lemma-covering-bound\">#</a></h1>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.wz_restrictedCoveringJoint_pos", ".././",
   "Restricted covering joint, full support. From a strictly\x0apositive row-stochastic kernel `κ'` and the source marginal `P_X x = ∑_y P_XY(x,y)`,\x0athe `(X, U)` joint `κ'(x, u) · P_X(x)` restricted to the support subtype\x0a`α' := {x // 0 < P_X x}` is a strictly positive pmf on `α' × Fin k`:\x0a\x0a* `α'` is nonempty (a probability measure cannot have every row of `P_X` vanish);\x0a* the joint is strictly positive on `α' × Fin k` (both factors are positive there);\x0a* it lies in the standard simplex (row-sums collapse to `∑_{x' : α'} P_X(x'.1) = 1`,\x0a  the zero atoms of `P_X` contributing nothing).\x0a\x0aThis is the global-full-support source the rate-distortion covering theorem\x0a`rate_distortion_achievability` hard-requires (`hqStar_pos`), obtained on the\x0arestricted alphabet because factorizability forces `P_X`'s zero atoms into the\x0ajoint regardless of `κ'`.\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>Restricted covering joint, full support. From a strictly\x0apositive row-stochastic kernel <code>κ'</code> and the source marginal <code>P_X x = ∑_y P_XY(x,y)</code>,\x0athe <code>(X, U)</code> joint <code>κ'(x, u) · P_X(x)</code> restricted to the support subtype\x0a<code>α' := {x // 0 &lt; P_X x}</code> is a strictly positive pmf on <code>α' × Fin k</code>:</p><ul><li><code>α'</code> is nonempty (a probability measure cannot have every row of <code>P_X</code> vanish);</li><li>the joint is strictly positive on <code>α' × Fin k</code> (both factors are positive there);</li><li>it lies in the standard simplex (row-sums collapse to <code>∑_{x' : α'} P_X(x'.1) = 1</code>,\x0athe zero atoms of <code>P_X</code> contributing nothing).</li></ul><p>This is the global-full-support source the rate-distortion covering theorem\x0a<code>rate_distortion_achievability</code> hard-requires (<code>hqStar_pos</code>), obtained on the\x0arestricted alphabet because factorizability forces <code>P_X</code>'s zero atoms into the\x0ajoint regardless of <code>κ'</code>.\x0a@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.wz_codebook_confusion_expectation_le", "../.././",
   "Codebook-restricted decoder confusion exponent. The\x0abinning-averaged probability that some codebook member `c₁.decoder m'` other than\x0athe true covering codeword shares the true bin and is jointly typical with `Y^n` is at\x0amost `M₁ · exp(−n · I(U;Y)) · M⁻¹`.\x0a\x0aRestricting the confusable set to the covering codebook is what achieves the Wyner–Ziv\x0arate. Binning all `u`-sequences would give the count `exp(n·H(U|Y))`, forcing the rate\x0adown to `H(U|Y)` — too weak; this bound instead restricts to the covering codebook\x0a(`M₁ = ⌈exp(n·I(X;U))⌉` members), so the alias count is `M₁` rather than\x0a`exp(n·H(U|Y))`. With `M = ⌈exp(n·R)⌉` bins, the bound is\x0a`M₁ · exp(−n·I(U;Y)) / M ≈ exp(n·(I(X;U) − I(U;Y) − R))`, which vanishes precisely\x0awhen `R > I(X;U) − I(Y;U)` — the Wyner–Ziv rate.\x0a\x0a`hmass` is the per-codeword joint-typicality mass upper bound\x0a`μ{codeword m' typical with Y^n} ≤ exp(−n·I_YU)` (the AEP bound for a covering codeword\x0aindependent of `Y^n`); `hcollision` is the binning-collision property\x0a`binMeas{f | f m' = f m} = M⁻¹` for distinct indices, mirroring `binning_collision_prob`.\x0aThe codebook-restricted union over `m' : Fin M₁` stays in the body (not a hypothesis):\x0aswap the order of integration, bound the per-`ω` `binMeas`-slice by union bound +\x0a`hcollision` as `M⁻¹ · #{m' typical}`, integrate over `μ`, then apply `hmass` to each of\x0athe `M₁` codewords to get `M⁻¹ · M₁ · exp(−n·I_YU)`. `hYs`/`htrueIdx` (measurability of\x0athe side-information block RV and of the covering index) are regularity preconditions for\x0athe Tonelli swap, supplied by the call site.\x0a\x0aImplementation note: the typical set is an abstract measurable set `jts` (parameter\x0a`hjts_meas : MeasurableSet jts`) rather than a concrete `jointlyTypicalSet`, since the\x0abody uses no property of it beyond measurability. This lets the call site instantiate the\x0aconfusion integral under the source product measure with the typical set defined on the\x0aside-information ambient — two different measures a concrete typical set could never match;\x0athe per-codeword mass `hmass` is then supplied via a side-information-marginal transfer to\x0a`wz_covering_codeword_sideInfo_mass_le`.\x0a@audit:ok ",
   "<p>Codebook-restricted decoder confusion exponent. The\x0abinning-averaged probability that some codebook member <code>c₁.decoder m'</code> other than\x0athe true covering codeword shares the true bin and is jointly typical with <code>Y^n</code> is at\x0amost <code>M₁ · exp(−n · I(U;Y)) · M⁻¹</code>.</p><p>Restricting the confusable set to the covering codebook is what achieves the Wyner–Ziv\x0arate. Binning all <code>u</code>-sequences would give the count <code>exp(n·H(U|Y))</code>, forcing the rate\x0adown to <code>H(U|Y)</code> — too weak; this bound instead restricts to the covering codebook\x0a(<code>M₁ = ⌈exp(n·I(X;U))⌉</code> members), so the alias count is <code>M₁</code> rather than\x0a<code>exp(n·H(U|Y))</code>. With <code>M = ⌈exp(n·R)⌉</code> bins, the bound is\x0a<code>M₁ · exp(−n·I(U;Y)) / M ≈ exp(n·(I(X;U) − I(U;Y) − R))</code>, which vanishes precisely\x0awhen <code>R &gt; I(X;U) − I(Y;U)</code> — the Wyner–Ziv rate.</p><p><code>hmass</code> is the per-codeword joint-typicality mass upper bound\x0a<code>μ{codeword m' typical with Y^n} ≤ exp(−n·I_YU)</code> (the AEP bound for a covering codeword\x0aindependent of <code>Y^n</code>); <code>hcollision</code> is the binning-collision property\x0a<code>binMeas{f | f m' = f m} = M⁻¹</code> for distinct indices, mirroring <code>binning_collision_prob</code>.\x0aThe codebook-restricted union over <code>m' : Fin M₁</code> stays in the body (not a hypothesis):\x0aswap the order of integration, bound the per-<code>ω</code> <code>binMeas</code>-slice by union bound +\x0a<code>hcollision</code> as <code>M⁻¹ · #{m' typical}</code>, integrate over <code>μ</code>, then apply <code>hmass</code> to each of\x0athe <code>M₁</code> codewords to get <code>M⁻¹ · M₁ · exp(−n·I_YU)</code>. <code>hYs</code>/<code>htrueIdx</code> (measurability of\x0athe side-information block RV and of the covering index) are regularity preconditions for\x0athe Tonelli swap, supplied by the call site.</p><p>Implementation note: the typical set is an abstract measurable set <code>jts</code> (parameter\x0a<code>hjts_meas : MeasurableSet jts</code>) rather than a concrete <code>jointlyTypicalSet</code>, since the\x0abody uses no property of it beyond measurability. This lets the call site instantiate the\x0aconfusion integral under the source product measure with the typical set defined on the\x0aside-information ambient — two different measures a concrete typical set could never match;\x0athe per-codeword mass <code>hmass</code> is then supplied via a side-information-marginal transfer to\x0a<code>wz_covering_codeword_sideInfo_mass_le</code>.\x0a@audit:ok</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.WynerZiv.Achievability.SourceTransport module doc 3", "./",
   "### The distortion-decomposition bridge\x0a\x0aThe bridge that the derandomize + squeeze glue consumes: it decomposes the Wyner–Ziv code's\x0aactual expected block distortion into a good-event proxy plus `distortionMax · Pr[error]`,\x0amirroring the rate-distortion `source_avg_distortion_le_simpler`\x0a(`AchievabilityAsymptoticFailureDecay.lean`) but for the bin conditional-typicality decoder\x0a(`wzBinTypicalDecoder`) threaded through `wzCodeOfCoveringBinning`.\x0a\x0a* `wz_expectedBlockDistortion_le_of_badSet` — the generic, decoder-agnostic\x0a  measure-theoretic decomposition (the reusable analytic core; sorry-free).\x0a* `wz_covering_binning_distortion_decomp` — the specialization to the covering+binning\x0a  code, splitting `Pr[error]` into the covering-distortion-failure event `E1` and the\x0a  bin-decoder confusion event `E2` (the shape the squeeze glue bounds).\x0a",
   "<h3 id=\"The-distortion-decomposition-bridge\" class=\"markdown-heading\">The distortion-decomposition bridge <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#The-distortion-decomposition-bridge\">#</a></h3><p>The bridge that the derandomize + squeeze glue consumes: it decomposes the Wyner–Ziv code's\x0aactual expected block distortion into a good-event proxy plus <code>distortionMax · Pr[error]</code>,\x0amirroring the rate-distortion <code>source_avg_distortion_le_simpler</code>\x0a(<code>AchievabilityAsymptoticFailureDecay.lean</code>) but for the bin conditional-typicality decoder\x0a(<code>wzBinTypicalDecoder</code>) threaded through <code>wzCodeOfCoveringBinning</code>.</p><ul><li><code>wz_expectedBlockDistortion_le_of_badSet</code> — the generic, decoder-agnostic\x0ameasure-theoretic decomposition (the reusable analytic core; sorry-free).</li><li><code>wz_covering_binning_distortion_decomp</code> — the specialization to the covering+binning\x0acode, splitting <code>Pr[error]</code> into the covering-distortion-failure event <code>E1</code> and the\x0abin-decoder confusion event <code>E2</code> (the shape the squeeze glue bounds).</li></ul>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.wzMarkovCrossEq", ".././",
   "Markov-chain cross-product form\x0a`q(x,y,u) · q_X(x,u') = q(x,y,u') · q_X(x,u)` where `q_X(x,u) := ∑_y q(x,y,u)`.\x0aEncoded as a real-valued affine condition (well-defined where `q_X` vanishes —\x0aboth sides become `0`). ",
   "<p>Markov-chain cross-product form\x0a<code>q(x,y,u) · q_X(x,u') = q(x,y,u') · q_X(x,u)</code> where <code>q_X(x,u) := ∑_y q(x,y,u)</code>.\x0aEncoded as a real-valued affine condition (well-defined where <code>q_X</code> vanishes —\x0aboth sides become <code>0</code>).</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.WynerZiv.Converse.Headline module doc 0", "../.././",
   "# Wyner–Ziv converse — endpoint continuity and the operational headline\x0a\x0aThe endpoint right-continuity infrastructure and the operational converse headline:\x0aan achievable Wyner–Ziv rate is bounded below by the single-letter rate–distortion function.\x0a",
   "<h1 id=\"Wyner-Ziv-converse-endpoint-continuity-and-the-operational-headline\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Wyner–Ziv converse — endpoint continuity and the operational headline <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Wyner-Ziv-converse-endpoint-continuity-and-the-operational-headline\">#</a></h1><p>The endpoint right-continuity infrastructure and the operational converse headline:\x0aan achievable Wyner–Ziv rate is bounded below by the single-letter rate–distortion function.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.WynerZiv.Converse.Prelim module doc 2", "./",
   "## Reshaped operational rate: non-degeneracy (data-processing lower bound)\x0a\x0aThe reshaped rate `wynerZivRate` (`FactorizableRate.lean` §10) is\x0a`sInf (wzRateValueSet …)`. Its honest non-degeneracy rests on the objective's\x0adata-processing non-negativity `I(X;U) − I(Y;U) ≥ 0` on the factorizable\x0amanifold (Markov chain `U − X − Y`), which discharges the `BddBelow` guard that\x0aprevents a junk `sInf` collapse to `≤ 0`. ",
   "<h2 id=\"Reshaped-operational-rate-non-degeneracy-data-processing-lower-bound\" class=\"markdown-heading\">Reshaped operational rate: non-degeneracy (data-processing lower bound) <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#Reshaped-operational-rate-non-degeneracy-data-processing-lower-bound\">#</a></h2><p>The reshaped rate <code>wynerZivRate</code> (<code>FactorizableRate.lean</code> §10) is\x0a<code>sInf (wzRateValueSet …)</code>. Its honest non-degeneracy rests on the objective's\x0adata-processing non-negativity <code>I(X;U) − I(Y;U) ≥ 0</code> on the factorizable\x0amanifold (Markov chain <code>U − X − Y</code>), which discharges the <code>BddBelow</code> guard that\x0aprevents a junk <code>sInf</code> collapse to <code>≤ 0</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.WynerZiv.FactorizableRate module doc 9", ".././",
   "## §9 Rate-level convex inequality (final wrapper) ",
   "<h2 id=\"9-Rate-level-convex-inequality-final-wrapper\" class=\"markdown-heading\">§9 Rate-level convex inequality (final wrapper) <a class=\"hover-link\" href=\"#9-Rate-level-convex-inequality-final-wrapper\">#</a></h2>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.mem_wzRateValueSet_iff", "../.././",
   "Membership in `wzRateValueSet`: a real `v` is a value iff it is the objective\x0aof a feasible factorizable point at some finite auxiliary alphabet `Fin k`. ",
   "<p>Membership in <code>wzRateValueSet</code>: a real <code>v</code> is a value iff it is the objective\x0aof a feasible factorizable point at some finite auxiliary alphabet <code>Fin k</code>.</p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.wzObjective_convex_of_condEntDiff", "./",
   "Objective convexity from the conditional-entropy-difference core.\x0aGiven `WynerZivCondEntDiffConvex` (the Lemma-15.9 core), the Wyner–Ziv\x0aobjective `I(X;U) − I(Y;U)` is convex along factorizable convex\x0acombinations — i.e. exactly the `h_obj_convex` hypothesis consumed by\x0a`WynerZiv/FactorizableRate.lean`.\x0a\x0a`@audit:superseded-by(wynerZivCondEntDiffConvex_holds)` ",
   "<p>Objective convexity from the conditional-entropy-difference core.\x0aGiven <code>WynerZivCondEntDiffConvex</code> (the Lemma-15.9 core), the Wyner–Ziv\x0aobjective <code>I(X;U) − I(Y;U)</code> is convex along factorizable convex\x0acombinations — i.e. exactly the <code>h_obj_convex</code> hypothesis consumed by\x0a<code><a href=\"./WynerZiv/FactorizableRate.html\">WynerZiv/FactorizableRate.lean</a></code>.</p><p><code>@audit:superseded-by(wynerZivCondEntDiffConvex_holds)</code></p>"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.wynerZivObjective_image_bddBelow", ".././",
   "The Wyner–Ziv objective image is bounded below. ",
   "<p>The Wyner–Ziv objective image is bounded below.</p>")]

