  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.OuterBound module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Broadcast channel — cooperative outer bound\x0a\x0aAn outer bound on the operational capacity region of a general two-receiver broadcast channel,\x0aobtained by letting the two receivers cooperate.  Each receiver alone, and the two receivers\x0apooling their outputs, form single-user channels, so the single-user converse applies three\x0atimes and bounds the two individual rates and their sum by the corresponding capacities.\x0a\x0aThe reductions are code transformations: freezing one message index turns a broadcast code into\x0aa single-user code for the other receiver over the marginal channel, and pairing the two message\x0aindices turns it into a single-user code over the channel with the output pair as its output.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `BroadcastCode.restrict₁` / `BroadcastCode.restrict₂` — the single-user code obtained by\x0a  freezing the message of the other receiver.\x0a* `BroadcastCode.coop` — the single-user code for the cooperative receiver that sees both\x0a  outputs and decodes the message pair.\x0a* `bcOuterRegionCoop W` — the cooperative outer region, the intersection of the three half\x0a  planes cut out by the capacities of the two marginal channels and of the channel itself.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `bc_capacity_subset_coop` — the operational capacity region is contained in the cooperative\x0a  outer region.\x0a",
   "[[\"header\",1,[[\"normal\",\"Broadcast channel — cooperative outer bound\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"An outer bound on the operational capacity region of a general two-receiver broadcast channel,\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"obtained by letting the two receivers cooperate.  Each receiver alone, and the two receivers\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"pooling their outputs, form single-user channels, so the single-user converse applies three\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"times and bounds the two individual rates and their sum by the corresponding capacities.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"The reductions are code transformations: freezing one message index turns a broadcast code into\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"a single-user code for the other receiver over the marginal channel, and pairing the two message\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"indices turns it into a single-user code over the channel with the output pair as its output.\"]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main definitions\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"BroadcastCode.restrict₁\"]],[\"normal\",\" / \"],[\"code\",[\"BroadcastCode.restrict₂\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the single-user code obtained by\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"freezing the message of the other receiver.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"BroadcastCode.coop\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the single-user code for the cooperative receiver that sees both\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"outputs and decodes the message pair.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bcOuterRegionCoop W\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the cooperative outer region, the intersection of the three half\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"planes cut out by the capacities of the two marginal channels and of the channel itself.\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main statements\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bc_capacity_subset_coop\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the operational capacity region is contained in the cooperative\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"outer region.\"]]]]]]]]"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.OuterBoundTransport module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Comparing the one- and two-auxiliary-receiver outer bounds of a broadcast channel\x0a\x0aTwo genie-aided outer bounds for the two-receiver discrete memoryless broadcast channel are\x0acompared constraint by constraint. The first bound hands a single auxiliary receiver output\x0a`J` to the genie and constrains the rate triple `(R₀, R₁, R₂)` through three systems of\x0aauxiliary variables — a plain one `(W, U, V)`, a first enhanced one `(W̃, Ũ, Ṽ)` and a second\x0aenhanced one `(Ŵ, Û, V̂)`. The second bound uses two auxiliary receiver outputs, one per\x0areceiver, and constrains the same triple through two systems.\x0a\x0aThe right-hand sides of the second bound are recorded here already specialized to the pair of\x0aauxiliary receivers `(const, X)`, where the input itself is handed to the second receiver and\x0anothing to the first. Under that choice the first system of the two-receiver bound drops out\x0aand each right-hand side collapses to a closed form in the second system alone. The\x0aspecialization is taken as a definition; deriving those closed forms from the general shape is\x0anot done here. Besides the substitution itself, some of its steps use the conditional\x0aindependence of each system from the receiver outputs given the input, which the bound builds\x0ainto its witnesses; the comparisons that need that structure again carry it as an explicit\x0ahypothesis.\x0a\x0aThe one-auxiliary-receiver bound constrains the sum rate twice over, once through the enhanced\x0asystems together with `J` and once through the plain system alone. Those two right-hand sides\x0adiffer by exactly the slack in one of the compatibility conditions the same bound imposes on its\x0awitnesses, so the first of the two constraints follows from the second and never binds.\x0a\x0aThe auxiliary receiver output can be eliminated altogether from the remaining right-hand sides.\x0aEach of them opens with a minimum of two branches, one reading `J` and one not, and the\x0acompatibility condition at the level of `W` forces the minimum onto the branch that does not; the\x0aconditions at the level of `U` and of `V` then rewrite whatever tail still reads `J` in terms of\x0athe plain system. So `J` survives only in the conditions that say which witnesses the bound\x0aadmits, not in the right-hand sides those witnesses produce.\x0a\x0aFeeding one and the same system to the plain slot and to both enhanced slots turns the\x0acompatibility conditions tying the three systems together into identities, and collapses the five\x0aright-hand sides on `R₀`, on `R₀ + R₁` and on `R₀ + R₂` onto the three that are stated through\x0athe plain system alone. With the sum-rate right-hand side, which is already stated through that\x0asystem, four of the constraints survive.\x0a\x0aTwo of the right-hand sides stated through the plain system alone, the one on `R₀ + R₁` and the\x0aone on `R₀ + R₁ + R₂`, can be weighted against each other. Reading the plain system's pair\x0a`(W, U)` as a single auxiliary variable bounds that weighting at every `t ≥ 0` by a single\x0aexpression whose first term is `I(W, U; Y)`: the chain rule at `(W, U)` absorbs the first user's\x0atail, and the sum-rate right-hand side is discarded onto the branch of its inner minimum that\x0acarries the same tail. Conditional independence of `(W, U)` from the second receiver output given\x0athe input, which the bound builds into its witnesses, then rewrites the remaining conditional\x0aterm as a difference of two mutual informations with that output.\x0a\x0aEverything below relates right-hand sides of constraints to one another. Turning such a relation\x0ainto a statement about the rate regions the two bounds cut out needs the constraints themselves,\x0aand that step is not taken here.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `singleAuxCommonBound`, `singleAuxFirstUserBound`, `singleAuxSecondUserBound` — the\x0a  right-hand sides of the one-auxiliary-receiver constraints on `R₀`, on `R₀ + R₁` and on\x0a  `R₀ + R₂` that participate in the comparison.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ` and `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ` — the companion constraints\x0a  on `R₀ + R₁` and on `R₀ + R₂`, whose leading minimum opens at the first enhanced system rather\x0a  than at the second.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBoundJFree`, `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZJFree`,\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBoundJFree`, `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZJFree` — the same four\x0a  right-hand sides written without the auxiliary receiver output.\x0a* `plainCommonBound`, `plainFirstUserBound`, `plainSecondUserBound` — the right-hand sides of the\x0a  one-auxiliary-receiver constraints on `R₀`, on `R₀ + R₁` and on `R₀ + R₂` that are stated\x0a  through the plain system alone.\x0a* `twoAuxCommonBound`, `twoAuxFirstUserBound`, `twoAuxFirstUserBoundInput`,\x0a  `twoAuxSecondUserBound`, `twoAuxSecondUserBoundInput` — the five right-hand sides of the\x0a  two-auxiliary-receiver bound at `(const, X)`. The `Input` suffix marks the two of them whose\x0a  leading term reads the channel input `X` where its partner reads a receiver output.\x0a* `enhancedSumRateBound` and `plainSumRateBound` — the two right-hand sides of the\x0a  one-auxiliary-receiver bound on `R₀ + R₁ + R₂`.\x0a* `secondUserSlack` — the slack in the upper half of the compatibility condition that ties the\x0a  second user's tail in the first enhanced system to the input.\x0a* `plainDirectionalCombination` — the weighting `(1 - t) · plainFirstUserBound +\x0a  t · plainSumRateBound` of the two plain right-hand sides that the direction `(0, 1, t)` pairs.\x0a* `plainDirectionalBound` and `plainDirectionalBoundCondFree` — the two forms of the upper bound\x0a  on that weighting, `I(W, U; Y) + t · I(X; Z | W, U)` and\x0a  `I(W, U; Y) + t · (I(X; Z) - I(W, U; Z))`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `singleAuxCommonBound_le_twoAuxCommonBound` — the `R₀` constraint transports.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBound_le_twoAuxFirstUserBound` and\x0a  `twoAuxFirstUserBound_le_twoAuxFirstUserBoundInput` — the two `R₀ + R₁` constraints.\x0a* `singleAuxSecondUserBound_le_twoAuxSecondUserBound` and\x0a  `twoAuxSecondUserBound_le_twoAuxSecondUserBoundInput` — the two `R₀ + R₂` constraints,\x0a  the first of them at the single instance `J := X` of the auxiliary receiver.\x0a* `condMutualInfoReal_sub_eq_condMutualInfoReal_prod` — the closed form\x0a  `I(Ṽ; X | W̃) - I(Ṽ; Z | W̃) = I(Ṽ; X | W̃, Z)` of the tail that the `R₀ + R₂` comparison\x0a  discards.\x0a* `enhancedSumRateBound_sub_plainSumRateBound_eq_secondUserSlack` — the two sum-rate right-hand\x0a  sides differ by `secondUserSlack`.\x0a* `plainSumRateBound_le_enhancedSumRateBound` and\x0a  `le_enhancedSumRateBound_of_le_plainSumRateBound` — the resulting order and the redundancy of\x0a  the sum-rate constraint that carries `J`.\x0a* `singleAuxFirstUserBound_eq_jFree`, `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ_eq_jFree`,\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBound_eq_jFree` and `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ_eq_jFree` — the four\x0a  right-hand sides that read the auxiliary receiver output equal ones that do not.\x0a* `singleAuxCommonBound_diagonal_eq_plainCommonBound`,\x0a  `singleAuxFirstUserBound_diagonal_eq_plainFirstUserBound`,\x0a  `singleAuxFirstUserBoundFromZ_diagonal_eq_plainFirstUserBound`,\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBound_diagonal_eq_plainSecondUserBound` and\x0a  `singleAuxSecondUserBoundFromZ_diagonal_eq_plainSecondUserBound` — feeding one system to all\x0a  three slots collapses five of the constraints onto the three stated through the plain system\x0a  alone, whatever the auxiliary receiver output is.\x0a* `plainDirectionalCombination_le_plainDirectionalBound` — the weighting of the two plain\x0a  right-hand sides at every `t ≥ 0` is at most `I(W, U; Y) + t · I(X; Z | W, U)`.\x0a* `plainDirectionalBound_eq_condFree` and\x0a  `plainDirectionalCombination_le_plainDirectionalBoundCondFree` — the conditional tail of that\x0a  upper bound is the difference `I(X; Z) - I(W, U; Z)` once `(W, U)` is conditionally independent\x0a  of the second receiver output given the input.\x0a\x0aNone of the five comparisons uses the compatibility conditions tying the three systems of the\x0aone-auxiliary-receiver bound together; the chain rule and the data processing inequality\x0asuffice. The sum-rate identity is the opposite case: two of those conditions enter it, together\x0awith a third that equates the two branches of the inner minimum of `plainSumRateBound`, and each\x0ais carried as an explicit hypothesis.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aThe constraints mix mutual informations additively and subtractively, so they are stated over\x0a`ℝ` rather than over `ℝ≥0∞`. Since `(⊤ : ℝ≥0∞).toReal = 0`, an infinite mutual information\x0awould silently satisfy an inequality it violates; every bridge to the `ℝ≥0∞`-valued chain rule\x0atherefore goes through an explicit finiteness fact, which the finite alphabets supply.\x0a\x0aEvery random variable is an explicit binder rather than a section variable: the alphabets are\x0afree type variables, so auto-bound implicits would let a permuted application elaborate into a\x0adifferent statement.\x0a",
