  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Superposition.MoreCapable module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Broadcast channel — the capacity region of a more capable channel\x0a\x0a`IsBCMoreCapable` compares the two marginal channels under every input law.  The converse for a\x0amore capable channel needs that comparison in two further shapes: conditioned on an auxiliary\x0avariable, and read on a five-tuple law of the UV outer region.  Both are averages of the\x0aunconditional statement over the conditional input laws, which is exactly what the mixture\x0aidentity for conditional mutual information delivers, so the comparison transports with no new\x0aanalysis.\x0a\x0aWhat the comparison buys is the sum-rate inequality `I(V; Y₁) + I(X; Y₂ ∣ V) ≤ I(X; Y₁)`, the\x0afourth constraint of the UV outer region, which the less noisy converse discards and the more\x0acapable one needs.  The companion bound at the receiver-2 corner, `I(U; Y₂) ≤ I(X; Y₁)`, is the\x0asame comparison composed with the data processing inequality along `U → X → Y₂`; it is what the\x0asum constraint degenerates to once the first rate is clamped at zero.\x0a\x0aThe right-hand side of both bounds is `uvInfoJoint`, the information the input carries about the\x0afirst output.  It is the slot of a five-tuple law that the two-constraint inner bound never\x0aneeded, and this file records the fact the full-support step asks of it beyond its invariance\x0aunder relabeling: it is concave in the law, so a positive multiple of it survives the mixture\x0athat repairs full support.\x0a\x0aKeeping that slot is what lets the inner bound keep the sum-rate constraint a general broadcast\x0achannel needs, instead of the two-constraint region that is exact only over a less noisy one.\x0aTime sharing between a rate pair and the corner where the cloud auxiliary is constant meets the\x0atwo separate constraints at once, and the sum constraint survives the segment because the\x0ainput-output slot is the same at both of its endpoints; perturbing the achievability pair the\x0asegment lands on toward the uniform law then repairs its support, at a cost one weight covers for\x0aall three slots at once.  Truncating the auxiliary of a law of the outer region moves that law\x0aonto a finite alphabet, and leaves the input-output slot alone as well, so both costs vanish\x0aalong a single sequence of indices and the inner bound, being a closure, recovers the rate pair\x0aitself.  Combined with the outer bound and with the achievability of the inner one, this\x0adescribes the capacity region of a more capable broadcast channel by a single-letter expression.\x0aIt subsumes the description of a less noisy one, `IsBCLessNoisy` being the stronger comparison.\x0a\x0aThe sum constraint is carried with the first rate clamped at zero, `max R₁ 0 + R₂`, which is the\x0aform the achievability theorem takes and the form under which the inner bound is achievable with\x0ano comparison between the two receivers.  A negative first rate is then met at the receiver-2\x0acorner rather than by the sum-rate slot of the outer region, which is where the second of the two\x0acomparison bounds is spent.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `IsBCMoreCapable.condMutualInfo_le` — over a more capable channel, the receiver-2 information\x0a  conditioned on the cloud auxiliary is at most the receiver-1 one.\x0a* `uvInfoSum₁_le_uvInfoJoint_of_moreCapable` — over a more capable channel, the fourth UV outer\x0a  slot of a channel law is at most `I(X; Y₁)`.\x0a* `uvInfo₂_le_uvInfoJoint_of_moreCapable` — over a more capable channel, the receiver-2 slot of a\x0a  channel law is at most `I(X; Y₁)`.\x0a* `mul_uvInfoJoint_le_uvInfoJoint_uvPerturbLaw` — perturbing a law toward the uniform one with\x0a  weight `lam` keeps at least the fraction `lam` of `I(X; Y₁)`.\x0a* `bc_moreCapable_uv_subset_superposition` — the UV outer region of a more capable channel is\x0a  contained in the three-constraint superposition inner bound.\x0a* `bc_moreCapable_capacity_eq_uv` — the single-letter characterization: the capacity region of a\x0a  more capable broadcast channel whose transition law gives every output pair positive mass is\x0a  its UV outer region `bcOuterRegionUV`.\x0a* `bc_degraded_capacity_eq_uv` — the same characterization for a physically degraded channel,\x0a  degradedness being the strongest of the three comparisons.\x0a* `bc_moreCapable_superposition_eq_capacity` — the same capacity region read off the\x0a  superposition inner bound instead of the outer bound.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aTransporting the comparison means exchanging the measure inside an information, which `rw` cannot\x0ado: `condMutualInfo` takes the finiteness instance on its measure argument, so rewriting that\x0aargument leaves a motive that is not type correct.  `condMutualInfo_congr_measure` performs the\x0aexchange by substitution instead, and `mutualInfo_congr_pair` is the coarser form, where the two\x0aambient measures may differ as long as the joint law of the compared pair agrees.\x0a`mutualInfo_compProd_out₁` and `mutualInfo_compProd_out₂` read a marginal channel off a\x0acomposition product, which is what puts the comparison in the `mutualInfoOfChannel` form\x0a`IsBCMoreCapable` is stated in.\x0a",
   "[[\"header\",1,[[\"normal\",\"Broadcast channel — the capacity region of a more capable channel\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"IsBCMoreCapable\"]],[\"normal\",\" compares the two marginal channels under every input law.  The converse for a\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"more capable channel needs that comparison in two further shapes: conditioned on an auxiliary\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"variable, and read on a five-tuple law of the UV outer region.  Both are averages of the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"unconditional statement over the conditional input laws, which is exactly what the mixture\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"identity for conditional mutual information delivers, so the comparison transports with no new\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"analysis.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"What the comparison buys is the sum-rate inequality \"],[\"code\",[\"I(V; Y₁) + I(X; Y₂ ∣ V) ≤ I(X; Y₁)\"]],[\"normal\",\", the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"fourth constraint of the UV outer region, which the less noisy converse discards and the more\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"capable one needs.  The companion bound at the receiver-2 corner, \"],[\"code\",[\"I(U; Y₂) ≤ I(X; Y₁)\"]],[\"normal\",\", is the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"same comparison composed with the data processing inequality along \"],[\"code\",[\"U → X → Y₂\"]],[\"normal\",\"; it is what the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"sum constraint degenerates to once the first rate is clamped at zero.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"The right-hand side of both bounds is \"],[\"code\",[\"uvInfoJoint\"]],[\"normal\",\", the information the input carries about the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"first output.  It is the slot of a five-tuple law that the two-constraint inner bound never\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"needed, and this file records the fact the full-support step asks of it beyond its invariance\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"under relabeling: it is concave in the law, so a positive multiple of it survives the mixture\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"that repairs full support.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"Keeping that slot is what lets the inner bound keep the sum-rate constraint a general broadcast\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"channel needs, instead of the two-constraint region that is exact only over a less noisy one.\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"Time sharing between a rate pair and the corner where the cloud auxiliary is constant meets the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"two separate constraints at once, and the sum constraint survives the segment because the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"input-output slot is the same at both of its endpoints; perturbing the achievability pair the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"segment lands on toward the uniform law then repairs its support, at a cost one weight covers for\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"all three slots at once.  Truncating the auxiliary of a law of the outer region moves that law\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"onto a finite alphabet, and leaves the input-output slot alone as well, so both costs vanish\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"along a single sequence of indices and the inner bound, being a closure, recovers the rate pair\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"itself.  Combined with the outer bound and with the achievability of the inner one, this\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"describes the capacity region of a more capable broadcast channel by a single-letter expression.\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"It subsumes the description of a less noisy one, \"],[\"code\",[\"IsBCLessNoisy\"]],[\"normal\",\" being the stronger comparison.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"The sum constraint is carried with the first rate clamped at zero, \"],[\"code\",[\"max R₁ 0 + R₂\"]],[\"normal\",\", which is the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"form the achievability theorem takes and the form under which the inner bound is achievable with\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"no comparison between the two receivers.  A negative first rate is then met at the receiver-2\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"corner rather than by the sum-rate slot of the outer region, which is where the second of the two\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"comparison bounds is spent.\"]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main statements\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"IsBCMoreCapable.condMutualInfo_le\"]],[\"normal\",\" — over a more capable channel, the receiver-2 information\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"conditioned on the cloud auxiliary is at most the receiver-1 one.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"uvInfoSum₁_le_uvInfoJoint_of_moreCapable\"]],[\"normal\",\" — over a more capable channel, the fourth UV outer\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"slot of a channel law is at most \"],[\"code\",[\"I(X; Y₁)\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"uvInfo₂_le_uvInfoJoint_of_moreCapable\"]],[\"normal\",\" — over a more capable channel, the receiver-2 slot of a\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"channel law is at most \"],[\"code\",[\"I(X; Y₁)\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"mul_uvInfoJoint_le_uvInfoJoint_uvPerturbLaw\"]],[\"normal\",\" — perturbing a law toward the uniform one with\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"weight \"],[\"code\",[\"lam\"]],[\"normal\",\" keeps at least the fraction \"],[\"code\",[\"lam\"]],[\"normal\",\" of \"],[\"code\",[\"I(X; Y₁)\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bc_moreCapable_uv_subset_superposition\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the UV outer region of a more capable channel is\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"contained in the three-constraint superposition inner bound.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bc_moreCapable_capacity_eq_uv\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the single-letter characte