  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Superposition.Region module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Broadcast channel — the superposition inner bound\x0a\x0aSuperposition coding sends a cloud center `U` to both receivers and a satellite `X` to the\x0afirst one only.  This file takes the union of its inner bound over the auxiliary alphabets, so\x0athat it can be compared with the outer bounds as a set.\x0a\x0aTwo unions are taken, over the same full-support indices and differing only in the constraints\x0acutting out each member.  Keeping the sum-rate constraint gives the general superposition bound,\x0aachievable over any broadcast channel; dropping it gives a superset, achievable as soon as the\x0afirst receiver decodes the cloud at least as well as the second one does — which is exactly what\x0a`IsBCLessNoisy` asks, physical degradedness is not needed — because `I(U; Y₁) ≥ I(U; Y₂)` makes\x0athe omitted inequality follow from the two kept ones.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `bcSuperpositionRegionNoSumRate W` — the superposition inner bound without its sum-rate\x0a  constraint, as a union over auxiliary alphabets, restricted to the full-support indices.\x0a* `bcSuperpositionRegionSumRate W` — the same union with the sum-rate constraint kept.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `bc_lessNoisy_achievability` — the superposition rate pairs of a less noisy channel are\x0a  achievable.\x0a* `bcSuperpositionRegionNoSumRate_subset_capacity` — the two-constraint inner bound sits inside\x0a  the operational capacity region of a less noisy channel.\x0a* `bcSuperpositionRegionSumRate_subset_capacity` — the three-constraint inner bound sits inside\x0a  the operational capacity region, for any broadcast channel.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aThe auxiliary alphabets range over `Marton.bcAuxAlphabet`, one cardinality at a time in the\x0auniverse of the input alphabet.  That universe is forced: `IsBCLessNoisy` quantifies its\x0aauxiliary variable over the universe of the input alphabet, so a member of the union has to live\x0athere for the class hypothesis to apply to it.\x0a",
   "[[\"header\",1,[[\"normal\",\"Broadcast channel — the superposition inner bound\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"Superposition coding sends a cloud center \"],[\"code\",[\"U\"]],[\"normal\",\" to both receivers and a satellite \"],[\"code\",[\"X\"]],[\"normal\",\" to the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"first one only.  This file takes the union of its inner bound over the auxiliary alphabets, so\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"that it can be compared with the outer bounds as a set.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"Two unions are taken, over the same full-support indices and differing only in the constraints\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"cutting out each member.  Keeping the sum-rate constraint gives the general superposition bound,\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"achievable over any broadcast channel; dropping it gives a superset, achievable as soon as the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"first receiver decodes the cloud at least as well as the second one does — which is exactly what\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"code\",[\"IsBCLessNoisy\"]],[\"normal\",\" asks, physical degradedness is not needed — because \"],[\"code\",[\"I(U; Y₁) ≥ I(U; Y₂)\"]],[\"normal\",\" makes\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"the omitted inequality follow from the two kept ones.\"]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main definitions\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bcSuperpositionRegionNoSumRate W\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the superposition inner bound without its sum-rate\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"constraint, as a union over auxiliary alphabets, restricted to the full-support indices.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bcSuperpositionRegionSumRate W\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the same union with the sum-rate constraint kept.\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main statements\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bc_lessNoisy_achievability\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the superposition rate pairs of a less noisy channel are\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"achievable.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bcSuperpositionRegionNoSumRate_subset_capacity\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the two-constraint inner bound sits inside\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"the operational capacity region of a less noisy channel.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"bcSuperpositionRegionSumRate_subset_capacity\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the three-constraint inner bound sits inside\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"the operational capacity region, for any broadcast channel.\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Implementation notes\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"The auxiliary alphabets range over \"],[\"code\",[\"Marton.bcAuxAlphabet\"]],[\"normal\",\", one cardinality at a time in the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"universe of the input alphabet.  That universe is forced: \"],[\"code\",[\"IsBCLessNoisy\"]],[\"normal\",\" quantifies its\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"auxiliary variable over the universe of the input alphabet, so a member of the union has to live\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"there for the class hypothesis to apply to it.\"]]]]"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.BroadcastChannel.Superposition.TimeShare module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Broadcast channel — absorbing a time-sharing variable into the superposition cloud\x0a\x0aA point of the UV outer region is described by a five-tuple law, whose two information slots\x0a`I(U; Y₂)` and `I(X; Y₁ ∣ U)` are the corners of a rectangle the superposition inner bound only\x0areaches after the rate pair has been traded along a segment.  Time sharing performs that trade:\x0athe auxiliary is kept with probability `lam` and collapsed to a constant with probability\x0a`1 - lam`, and both branches are carried by the single auxiliary `Bool × U`, whose first\x0acomponent records which branch was taken.\x0a\x0aBecause the tag is recoverable from the auxiliary, the mixture identities for the two slots are\x0aexact where they need to be.  The receiver-2 corner only needs the branch that keeps the\x0aauxiliary, so the tag's own contribution is discarded and the slot is bounded below by\x0a`lam * I(U; Y₂)`.  The satellite slot is an equality, `lam * I(X; Y₁ ∣ U) + (1 - lam) * I(X; Y₁)`,\x0abecause collapsing the auxiliary turns the conditional information into the unconditional one.\x0aThe second endpoint therefore contributes `I(X; Y₁)`, and the segment traced by `lam` stays above\x0athe outer point as soon as the rate pair also satisfies `max R₁ 0 + R₂ ≤ I(X; Y₁)`.  That sum\x0aconstraint is all the assembly asks for beyond the three outer inequalities, and it comes for free\x0aover a less noisy channel, which forces `I(X; Y₁ ∣ U) + I(U; Y₂) ≤ I(X; Y₁)` at every law.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `uvCloudLaw ν` and `uvSatelliteKernel ν` — the achievability pair read off a five-tuple law.\x0a* `uvTagTrue ν`, `uvTagFalse σ`, `uvMixKernel ν σ` and `uvMixLaw ν σ lam` — two channel laws\x0a  mixed along a Bernoulli tag carried by the auxiliary, each of them keeping its auxiliary under\x0a  its own tag.\x0a* `uvCollapse ν u₀` and `uvTagConst ν u₀` — the law with its auxiliary collapsed to the constant\x0a  `u₀`, and its false-tagged copy.\x0a* `uvTimeShareLaw ν u₀ lam` — the law and its own collapsed copy mixed with weight `lam`.\x0a* `boolProdAuxEquiv m` — the tagged auxiliary alphabet re-encoded into `Marton.bcAuxAlphabet`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `bcInfo₂_uvCloudLaw`, `bcInfo₁_uvCloudLaw` and `bcInfoJoint_uvCloudLaw` — the three\x0a  informations of the achievability pair read off a channel law are the corresponding slots of\x0a  that law.\x0a* `mul_uvInfo₂_le_uvInfo₂_uvMixLaw` and `mul_condMutualInfo_le_condMutualInfo_uvMixLaw` — the two\x0a  slots of a mixture of two channel laws against the slots of the branch it keeps.\x0a* `mul_uvInfo₂_le_uvInfo₂_uvTimeShareLaw` and `condMutualInfo_uvTimeShareLaw` — the two slots of\x0a  the time-shared law against the slots of the original one.\x0a* `uvInfoJoint_uvTimeShareLaw` — time sharing leaves the input-output information `I(X; Y₁)`\x0a  unchanged.\x0a* `exists_bcInfo_ge_sumRate_of_isUVChannelLaw` — a rate pair satisfying the three UV outer\x0a  inequalities of a channel law together with the sum constraint `max R₁ 0 + R₂ ≤ I(X; Y₁)` is\x0a  dominated by the three informations of some achievability pair, with no comparison between the\x0a  two receivers.\x0a* `exists_bcInfo_ge_of_lessNoisy_of_isUVChannelLaw` — over a less noisy channel, a rate pair\x0a  satisfying the three UV outer inequalities of a channel law is dominated by the two\x0a  informations of some achievability pair, the sum constraint coming for free from the\x0a  comparison.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0a`uvMixLaw` mixes two arbitrary channel laws along the tag rather than a law and its own collapsed\x0acopy, because the same construction carries the perturbation toward the uniform law as well:\x0aforgetting the tag turns `uvMixLaw ν (uvUniformLaw W v₀) lam` into `uvPerturbLaw W ν v₀ lam`.\x0aTime sharing is the case where the second law is `uvCollapse ν u₀`, so `uvTimeShareLaw` is defined\x0aas that specialization and both mixtures share the lemmas about the tag.\x0a",
