  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.MutualInfoReencoding module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Invariance of an information slot under a re-encoding of one of its variables\x0a\x0aAn information slot depends on the variable filling it only through the information that variable\x0acarries, so replacing the variable by its image under a map that has a left inverse leaves the slot\x0aunchanged: the data processing inequality bounds the slot in one direction along the map and in the\x0aother along the left inverse.  The same holds for a variable replaced by one equal to it almost\x0aeverywhere, since the two induce the same joint law.  For a re-encoding of the conditioning\x0avariable the chain rule turns the statement about a pair back into one about the conditional\x0amutual information.\x0a\x0aNone of the statements mentions a channel or a code: they are properties of `mutualInfo` and\x0a`condMutualInfo` under a substitution of one of their variables.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse` — re-encoding a variable by a map that has a left inverse leaves\x0a  the mutual information it carries about another variable unchanged.\x0a* `mutualInfo_congr_ae` — two variables that agree almost everywhere carry the same mutual\x0a  information about a third.\x0a* `condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond` — the same re-encoding applied to the conditioning\x0a  variable of a conditional mutual information, obtained from the chain rule by cancelling the\x0a  tag term on both sides.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0aA variable relabeled by a `MeasurableEquiv` is the special case `g := e.symm`, so\x0a`mutualInfo_map_left_measurableEquiv` is an instance of `mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse`; it is\x0astated separately because it is proved directly from `klDiv_map_measurableEquiv`, without the\x0adata processing inequality.  Its conditioner analogue `condMutualInfo_map_cond_measurableEquiv`\x0ais not subsumed the same way: cancelling the conditioning variable's own term of the chain rule,\x0awhich is how `condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond` is proved, needs that term to be finite,\x0awhereas a relabel needs no side condition at all.\x0a",
   "[[\"header\",1,[[\"normal\",\"Invariance of an information slot under a re-encoding of one of its variables\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"An information slot depends on the variable filling it only through the information that variable\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"carries, so replacing the variable by its image under a map that has a left inverse leaves the slot\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"unchanged: the data processing inequality bounds the slot in one direction along the map and in the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"other along the left inverse.  The same holds for a variable replaced by one equal to it almost\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"everywhere, since the two induce the same joint law.  For a re-encoding of the conditioning\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"variable the chain rule turns the statement about a pair back into one about the conditional\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"mutual information.\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"None of the statements mentions a channel or a code: they are properties of \"],[\"code\",[\"mutualInfo\"]],[\"normal\",\" and\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"code\",[\"condMutualInfo\"]],[\"normal\",\" under a substitution of one of their variables.\"]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main statements\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse\"]],[\"normal\",\" — re-encoding a variable by a map that has a left inverse leaves\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"the mutual information it carries about another variable unchanged.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"mutualInfo_congr_ae\"]],[\"normal\",\" — two variables that agree almost everywhere carry the same mutual\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"information about a third.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the same re-encoding applied to the conditioning\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"variable of a conditional mutual information, obtained from the chain rule by cancelling the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"tag term on both sides.\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Implementation notes\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"A variable relabeled by a \"],[\"code\",[\"MeasurableEquiv\"]],[\"normal\",\" is the special case \"],[\"code\",[\"g := e.symm\"]],[\"normal\",\", so\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"code\",[\"mutualInfo_map_left_measurableEquiv\"]],[\"normal\",\" is an instance of \"],[\"code\",[\"mutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse\"]],[\"normal\",\"; it is\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"stated separately because it is proved directly from \"],[\"code\",[\"klDiv_map_measurableEquiv\"]],[\"normal\",\", without the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"data processing inequality.  Its conditioner analogue \"],[\"code\",[\"condMutualInfo_map_cond_measurableEquiv\"]],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"is not subsumed the same way: cancelling the conditioning variable's own term of the chain rule,\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"which is how \"],[\"code\",[\"condMutualInfo_eq_of_leftInverse_cond\"]],[\"normal\",\" is proved, needs that term to be finite,\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"whereas a relabel needs no side condition at all.\"]]]]"),
