  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.ShannonHartley.Achievability module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Continuous-time Shannon-Hartley: achievability (Cover-Thomas Ch. 9.6)\x0a\x0aThe `≥` half of the operational Shannon-Hartley sandwich,\x0a\x0a    `bandlimitedAwgnCapacity W N₀ P ≤ contAwgnOperationalCapacity W N₀ P`\x0a\x0a(`contAwgn_ge_shannonHartley`), together with the boundedness obligation\x0a`contAwgnMaxMessages_bddAbove` that the operational `sSup` needs in order not to collapse to\x0ajunk-`0`.\x0a\x0aThe two have different characters, and the split is the point. Boundedness holds\x0aunconditionally: Bessel's inequality against the orthonormal `ContAwgnCode.testFn` caps the\x0aobserved energy by `T·P` uniformly in the observation count, which is enough for `BddAbove` but\x0aonly enough for the crude rate `P/N₀`. Achievability at the *exact* constant needs more: the\x0a`≈ 2WT` degrees-of-freedom count — the time–band-limiting operator's eigenvalue concentration\x0a(Landau–Pollak–Slepian) — because the test family must recover near-unit gain on `≈ 2WT`\x0adimensions. See the two declarations for detail.\x0a\x0a## The synthesis bridge\x0a\x0a`synthSignal T n a` reconstructs a band-limited signal interpolating the sample values\x0a`a : Fin n → ℝ` at the grid `t_i = i·(T/n)`; it is how the band-limited codewords of a\x0a`ContAwgnCode` get built from a discrete `awgn_achievability` codebook. Its three properties power\x0athe reduction:\x0a\x0a* interpolation exactness — `synthSignal T n a (j·(T/n)) = a j`\x0a  (`synthSignal_sample`).\x0a* band-limitedness — `IsBandlimited (synthSignal T n a) W` when `n ≤ 2WT`\x0a  (`synthSignal_bandlimited`): each shifted `sincN(·/Δ)` has spectrum supported in\x0a  `[-1/(2Δ), 1/(2Δ)] = [-n/(2T), n/(2T)] ⊆ [-W, W]`.\x0a* Parseval energy — `∫ t, (synthSignal T n a t)² = (T/n)·∑ᵢ (a i)²`\x0a  (`synthSignal_energy`), an equality on the *whole line*, which is exactly the shape\x0a  `ContAwgnCode.encoder_power` asks for: with `a = √(n/T)·c` it reads `∑ᵢ cᵢ² ≤ T·P`.\x0a\x0a## References\x0a\x0a* T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, *Elements of Information Theory* (2nd ed.), Wiley, 2006.\x0a  Theorem 9.6.1 (achievability).\x0a",
