  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.Stein.Achievability module doc 0", 8044,
   "# Stein's lemma: achievability\x0a\x0aStein's lemma for binary hypothesis testing (Cover–Thomas, Theorem 11.8.3): the optimal\x0atype-II error of an `n`-sample test with type-I error at most `ε` decays exponentially in `n`\x0aat the rate of the Kullback–Leibler divergence `klDiv P Q`. This file develops the achievability\x0a(lower bound) side: the log-likelihood-ratio plumbing, the Stein-typical set, and the\x0atensorization of the KL divergence.\x0a\x0a## Main definitions\x0a\x0a* `llrPmf P Q` — the alphabet-side log-likelihood ratio `log P{x} − log Q{x}`.\x0a* `logLikelihoodRatio P Q Xs i` — the per-sample log-likelihood ratio along a process `Xs`.\x0a* `steinTypicalSet P Q n ε` — blocks whose empirical log-likelihood ratio is within `ε` of\x0a  `klDiv P Q`.\x0a\x0a## Main statements\x0a\x0a* `stein_strong_law` — the empirical mean of the log-likelihood ratio converges almost surely\x0a  to `(klDiv P Q).toReal`.\x0a* `stein_inProbability` — the same convergence holds in probability.\x0a* `stein_achievability` — there exist `ε`-level tests whose type-II error decays at rate\x0a  `klDiv P Q`.\x0a\x0a## Implementation notes\x0a\x0a* The log-likelihood-ratio plumbing is obtained as the two-distribution specialization of the\x0a  AEP development, which lets most measurability, identical-distribution, and independence\x0a  lemmas be reused rather than reproved.\x0a* Over a finite alphabet `α` with `0 < Q.real {x}` for every `x`, all quantities are expanded\x0a  point-wise, avoiding the general Radon–Nikodym derivative machinery for product measures.\x0a\x0a## References\x0a\x0a* T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, *Elements of Information Theory* (2nd ed.),\x0a  Wiley, 2006. Theorem 11.8.3.\x0a",
   "[[\"header\",1,[[\"normal\",\"Stein's lemma: achievability\"]]],[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"Stein's lemma for binary hypothesis testing (Cover–Thomas, Theorem 11.8.3): the optimal\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"type-II error of an \"],[\"code\",[\"n\"]],[\"normal\",\"-sample test with type-I error at most \"],[\"code\",[\"ε\"]],[\"normal\",\" decays exponentially in \"],[\"code\",[\"n\"]],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"at the rate of the Kullback–Leibler divergence \"],[\"code\",[\"klDiv P Q\"]],[\"normal\",\". This file develops the achievability\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"(lower bound) side: the log-likelihood-ratio plumbing, the Stein-typical set, and the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"tensorization of the KL divergence.\"]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main definitions\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"llrPmf P Q\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the alphabet-side log-likelihood ratio \"],[\"code\",[\"log P{x} − log Q{x}\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"logLikelihoodRatio P Q Xs i\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the per-sample log-likelihood ratio along a process \"],[\"code\",[\"Xs\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"steinTypicalSet P Q n ε\"]],[\"normal\",\" — blocks whose empirical log-likelihood ratio is within \"],[\"code\",[\"ε\"]],[\"normal\",\" of\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"code\",[\"klDiv P Q\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Main statements\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"stein_strong_law\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the empirical mean of the log-likelihood ratio converges almost surely\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"to \"],[\"code\",[\"(klDiv P Q).toReal\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"stein_inProbability\"]],[\"normal\",\" — the same convergence holds in probability.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"code\",[\"stein_achievability\"]],[\"normal\",\" — there exist \"],[\"code\",[\"ε\"]],[\"normal\",\"-level tests whose type-II error decays at rate\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"code\",[\"klDiv P Q\"]],[\"normal\",\".\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"Implementation notes\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"The log-likelihood-ratio plumbing is obtained as the two-distribution specialization of the\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"AEP development, which lets most measurability, identical-distribution, and independence\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"lemmas be reused rather than reproved.\"]]]]],[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"Over a finite alphabet \"],[\"code\",[\"α\"]],[\"normal\",\" with \"],[\"code\",[\"0 < Q.real {x}\"]],[\"normal\",\" for every \"],[\"code\",[\"x\"]],[\"normal\",\", all quantities are expanded\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"point-wise, avoiding the general Radon–Nikodym derivative machinery for product measures.\"]]]]]]],[\"header\",2,[[\"normal\",\"References\"]]],[\"ul\",true,\"*\",[[false,null,null,[[\"p\",[[\"normal\",\"T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas, \"],[\"em\",[[\"normal\",\"Elements of Information Theory\"]]],[\"normal\",\" (2nd ed.),\"],[\"softbr\",\"\\n\"],[\"normal\",\"Wiley, 2006. Theorem 11.8.3.\"]]]]]]]]"),
  ("InformationTheory.Shannon.stein_inProbability", 8044,
